WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.593 --> 00:00:04.980
Welcome to Ear Biscuits,

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.190
the podcast where two lifelong
friends talk about life

00:00:08.190 --> 00:00:09.810
for a long time.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:10.710
I'm Link.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:11.543
And I'm Rhett.

00:00:11.543 --> 00:00:13.700
This week at the round
table of dim lighting,

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.710
we are going to be pulling
back another curtain.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.620
And behind that curtain is two men

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.360
who have been going to therapy
for a certain length of time.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.497
And we're just gonna do a little check-in.

00:00:26.497 --> 00:00:27.330
I thought you were gonna say

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.163
you're gonna pull back that curtain,

00:00:28.163 --> 00:00:30.440
and it's two men and it's our therapists.

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:31.460
They're not joining us today.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.293
They're not joining us.

00:00:32.293 --> 00:00:35.160
We can say anything we
want about our therapist.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.470
But we won't. Well, we will.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:42.470
We're gonna be talking about
our experience thus far

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:45.080
in therapy and how we're viewing that,

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.037
what kind of stuff is happening.

00:00:47.037 --> 00:00:49.750
And let me, just in case
you're immediately like,

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:50.973
I don't care.

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:53.470
I'm not from Los Angeles.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.120
You guys obviously are.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.700
Listen, therapy's not just
for boys in Los Angeles.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.483
Therapy can be for anybody.

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.430
We, and it's been beneficial to us.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:07.200
We don't know if any, we
don't know if you think that,

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.610
but we used to think that.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.993
Yeah, we're speaking to our former selves.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.290
And we've, therapy's come
up a lot in conversations

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.630
because we're talking
about processing our lives,

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:24.523
and therapy is a big part of
that, an important part of it.

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:26.383
But we thought-

00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:28.107
You have a hair that-

00:01:28.107 --> 00:01:29.270
Oh, I can see it.

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:30.103
There we go.

00:01:30.103 --> 00:01:31.420
I know you don't like when hairs-

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:32.253
How's that?

00:01:32.253 --> 00:01:33.250
Are in that place or that place.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:34.083
How's that?

00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:35.040
And if people don't point
it out, you get upset.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.380
So I'm just letting you know right now.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:37.700
I don't want you to be
looking back at this

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.740
and be like, why did you let
me do that the whole time?

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.955
I'd hate for my therapist to
be the one to bring it up.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:48.260
But what we haven't done is
devote an extended conversation

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:51.270
to the topic in some time, if ever.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.523
So now's that time.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.253
I will say, I have a little update,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.420
recent travel excursion to.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:03.463
I mean, thankfully,

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:10.330
certain places in the
country have kind of,

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:11.460
we're not out of the pandemic,

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.510
but certain, there's certain places,

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:15.260
and if you take certain precautions,

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:19.400
you can feel somewhat safe to travel.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.080
I've kinda taken advantage of that.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.070
But also this was a wedding of a friend

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:24.670
that took place in Kansas City

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.270
that I was gonna go to regardless.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.610
But thankfully I could sort
of feel safe in doing so.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.960
But the most notable thing,
besides a beautiful wedding,

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.330
beautiful couple getting married

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:37.750
and beginning a beautiful life together,

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.120
which is not what I'm gonna talk about,

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.530
is the fact that we
stayed at a haunted hotel.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:44.620
Okay.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.185
Now, you know me.

00:02:46.185 --> 00:02:48.650
I'm into horror stuff.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.330
I like the idea of haunting.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:55.873
I'm pretty skeptical about the paranormal.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.430
I can act like I'm really into it,

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:02.530
but in my heart of hearts, in
my heart of hearts, I'm just,

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.563
this is probably all made up, right?

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.670
But that did not stop me
from being very enthusiastic

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.360
about the ghost tour.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:11.960
And this is coming from
someone who's been on

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:12.793
a few ghost tours.

00:03:12.793 --> 00:03:14.760
It's hard for me to get enthusiastic.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.647
The two that I went on with you,

00:03:16.647 --> 00:03:18.322
and one in New Orleans,

00:03:18.322 --> 00:03:19.230
and where was the other one?

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:20.063
London.

00:03:20.063 --> 00:03:21.720
It was a Jack the Ripper tour.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:22.693
That's right.

00:03:24.917 --> 00:03:26.000
Oh, boy, I just,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.010
I kinda needed to just
turn in for the night

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:28.890
about halfway through.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.230
Well, let me tell you
why I was particularly

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:32.960
excited about this one.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.010
Both of those ghost tours
were going around a city,

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.160
and then like coming up to a corner,

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.080
and looking at a building,

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.560
and hearing somebody who had
just been told information,

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.110
what to say about this thing.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:49.049
Are you telling me you had an
actual ghost give the tour?

00:03:49.049 --> 00:03:49.882
Well, it wasn't that good,

00:03:49.882 --> 00:03:53.160
but it's somewhere between the
original experience and that.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.110
Which is?

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:58.940
A ghost tour of a hotel
where you are staying.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:00.030
First of all-
Okay.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.920
We're in a different category.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:05.050
And secondly, well, there's
actually three points to this.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:05.890
The second point is-

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:06.930
Uh-oh, the points are expanding.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:08.510
The guy doing the tour,

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:10.310
and this isn't something
I knew ahead of time,

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.110
but the guy doing the tour

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:14.990
wasn't just talking about things
that other people had said.

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:17.520
He was telling his own stories

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.230
in being an employee for
15 years at the hotel.

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:23.180
So many of his stories were
like, this happened to me.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:25.290
Again, never experienced that.

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.593
And then the third thing was,

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:33.800
the bride and groom had experienced

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:40.610
some metaphysical unexplained
phenomena, you might say.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:41.930
Okay, what?

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.560
The first night in the hotel.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.210
Well, when you're
contemplating a marriage.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:47.050
The night before.

00:04:47.050 --> 00:04:48.647
Well, the night before the ghost tour.

00:04:48.647 --> 00:04:50.510
Things could go really wild when-

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.140
The night before the ghost tour.

00:04:52.140 --> 00:04:53.020
So...

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.510
Oh, and also Locke told
me that something happened

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:58.343
in his room.

00:04:58.343 --> 00:04:59.630
I'll tell you about that in a second.

00:04:59.630 --> 00:05:04.630
Apparently they wake up,

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:09.500
and the bedside table little
deal where the drawer,

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:10.333
it's a drawer.

00:05:10.333 --> 00:05:11.800
What would you call a bedside little thing

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:13.440
that's got a drawer and it's a table?

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:14.273
Bedside table.

00:05:14.273 --> 00:05:16.290
My bedside table's got two drawers, man.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:17.850
My previous one had three drawers.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.210
I don't think that's technically a table.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:21.520
Like in furniture speak,
I don't think it's a table

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:22.353
if it's got a drawer.

00:05:22.353 --> 00:05:23.930
Bedside chest of drawers?

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:24.870
It's definitely not that.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:26.170
There's probably like
a French word for it.

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:28.550
I'd like to get to the haunted part.

00:05:28.550 --> 00:05:32.220
The drawer had opened in the night, okay?

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:33.600
Okay, well, that could happen.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.610
They closed the drawer,

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:39.240
and the drawer opens again
before their very eyes.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:41.420
Now, this could be like
the gravity of the,

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:42.930
it could be like the floor.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:43.763
It's an old hotel.

00:05:43.763 --> 00:05:46.620
Like the gravity can open the drawers.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:47.603
Yeah.

00:05:47.603 --> 00:05:52.603
But then the, a water bottle,

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.947
like a, whatever, the one that you have.

00:05:55.947 --> 00:05:57.430
The one everybody's got.

00:05:57.430 --> 00:05:58.400
Like a Nalgene?

00:05:58.400 --> 00:05:59.820
No, the like Hydro Flask.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:00.653
Hydro Flask, okay.

00:06:00.653 --> 00:06:04.180
So like a metal water bottle
was on that same table,

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:08.350
and it, at some point in this process,

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:09.960
began to do that thing where

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:12.290
you know like a bottle
isn't quite settling,

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:15.920
and it's kinda like, kinda
rotating a little bit,

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:18.150
like right before, like a coin
settling and then stopping.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:19.760
Okay, yeah.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:20.810
That was happening,

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.193
but it was just happening indefinitely.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.540
It was just like, okay, this
bottle is gonna do this.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:28.020
They're just watching it.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.240
And then they stopped
it, and put it back down,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.043
and it started doing it again.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:35.453
And then they were just like, okay,

00:06:35.453 --> 00:06:38.060
you can have your drawer open.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:39.910
I don't know exactly
the sequence of events,

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:41.790
but 'cause I kinda heard
it as they were telling

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:42.623
a group of people.

00:06:42.623 --> 00:06:45.040
And I was, it was enough
for me to be freaked out.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:46.890
I have an explanation for that.

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:49.330
There's vibration in the hotel from like-

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:50.163
Hey, listen.

00:06:50.163 --> 00:06:50.996
The laundry room.

00:06:50.996 --> 00:06:51.900
There's always an explanation.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:53.760
Or the elevator shaft.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:54.593
I told you I'm a skeptic, man.

00:06:54.593 --> 00:06:57.510
Vibration takes care of
everything I've heard so far.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:58.690
But of course,

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:00.400
and every single thing I'm gonna tell you,

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:01.500
I'm not gonna tell you
everything that happens

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:02.333
on the ghost tour,

00:07:02.333 --> 00:07:04.620
but everything, every
single thing I was told,

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:06.830
because like Locke and
Shepherd, after the ghost store,

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:09.360
because Locke had heard
somebody whispering in the vent

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.390
at like three a.m. in
Locke and Shepherd's room.

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:16.010
And then he actually told the
guy on the ghost tour that,

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:17.250
the guy giving the tour.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:19.010
And he was like, oh, that's
just because all our,

00:07:19.010 --> 00:07:20.250
the whole system is connected.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:21.910
So you just heard somebody
whispering in another room.

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:23.350
He wasn't like feeding into it.

00:07:23.350 --> 00:07:25.310
That's good.
Maintaining credibility.

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:26.670
He's kinda like the Pope.

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:30.090
He's like, I'm the only one
who can tell ghost stories.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:31.990
Well, no, 'cause he was,

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:34.800
he had many people on the tour

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:37.400
were saying things that
had happened to them,

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.240
and he was validating some things.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:41.420
But if it was, if he actually
thought it was the truth-

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:42.420
He's keeping it real.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:44.760
But when Locke and Shepard
were, after the tour,

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:46.400
they were like, Dad, what
do you think about this?

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:49.710
I was like, well, you wanna
know what I really think?

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:52.350
I was scared at multiple
points on that ghost tour,

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:55.109
especially the moment when
we were standing at the

00:07:55.109 --> 00:07:56.570
top of the stairs,

00:07:56.570 --> 00:08:00.070
and some little girls who apparently knew

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:03.060
that the ghost tour comes by their room,

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:07.290
and they could hear the guy talking.

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:08.254
Jay is his name.

00:08:08.254 --> 00:08:12.700
He's actually, he's wearing one
of those headset microphones

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:14.370
with a speaker that's around his neck,

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:15.330
like a tour guide does.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:16.163
You know what I'm saying?

00:08:16.163 --> 00:08:17.510
So that he can just
speak in a normal volume,

00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:18.940
but everyone can hear him.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:20.410
So he's like coming by your room,

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:21.520
you know. oh, Jay's there.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:23.060
He's doing the tour.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:25.920
They waited until he was
in the middle of the story,

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:26.753
and they started going.

00:08:26.753 --> 00:08:28.590
And he had already just told us a story

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:31.130
about somebody being in a room
and seeing the handle move.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:32.020
Oh.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:33.700
And so then he's telling us a story,

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:36.693
and that's when these little
girls in the room started,

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:39.387
like taking their door
and like shaking it.

00:08:39.387 --> 00:08:40.710
And me, and Locke, and Shepherd

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:42.580
were like standing a
little bit down the hall,

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:45.423
and we immediately run up
next to the tour guide.

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:47.780
Before you have time to process,

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:49.920
oh, this is probably somebody
just playing a trick,

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.350
but I still, was just legitimately scared.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:54.250
Did the girls come out and say, gotcha?

00:08:54.250 --> 00:08:56.180
They just laughed from within the room,

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.940
which actually was sort of creepy,

00:08:57.940 --> 00:08:59.963
little girl laughter through a door.

00:09:00.830 --> 00:09:03.210
But I said, having said all that,

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:07.020
I believe that if you just, if you put me,

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:08.650
put my back against the
wall and telling me,

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:10.620
I've gotta give you an honest answer,

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:14.560
I think there's probably a
natural explanation for all this.

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:16.210
There's a reason that it happens at night.

00:09:16.210 --> 00:09:18.380
There's a reason that it
happens during the so-called

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:20.640
witching hour after three a.m.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:22.510
That's because you're
usually, you've been asleep,

00:09:22.510 --> 00:09:23.650
and you're waking up, and you're not,

00:09:23.650 --> 00:09:24.960
your senses aren't complete.

00:09:24.960 --> 00:09:27.060
You're just pouring water on yourself now.

00:09:28.624 --> 00:09:29.457
You okay?

00:09:29.457 --> 00:09:30.580
It was haunted, man.

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:31.760
My tea was haunted.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.130
So in other words, what I'm saying is that

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:36.020
I think that there's probably

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:39.010
almost always a natural explanation,

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:41.070
but I'm not sure.

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:42.037
I'm open to the fact that places-

00:09:42.037 --> 00:09:44.420
That's not fun.
Places can be haunted,

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:47.850
and it's more fun to believe
that the place is haunted.

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.550
So, and this guy, Jay, he's.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:52.250
Oh, the other thing that happened.

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:58.810
A lot of famous people
have been to this hotel.

00:09:58.810 --> 00:10:01.130
In fact, the room that the
bride and groom are staying in

00:10:01.130 --> 00:10:03.830
is the room that Harry Truman slept in

00:10:03.830 --> 00:10:06.730
the night that he found out
that he won the presidency.

00:10:06.730 --> 00:10:09.250
He went to sleep thinking
that he had lost,

00:10:09.250 --> 00:10:10.920
and they woke him up at
like two a.m. and said,

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:11.753
sir, you won.

00:10:11.753 --> 00:10:13.240
And then there's another room that

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:14.860
is a room where like Al Capone

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:16.340
would come and play cards and stuff.

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:17.540
And they were like, he
would sit right here,

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:20.260
and he could see this through
this window and this window.

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:22.740
He had both entrances covered
in case the feds came in,

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.680
he could go out this way or whatever.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:26.150
So it's been around 100 years.

00:10:26.150 --> 00:10:27.690
It's got this storied history.

00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:28.523
Where is it?

00:10:28.523 --> 00:10:30.350
It is outside of Kansas City, Missouri.

