00:20:45.250 --> 00:20:49.190
that it kinda finds way to get out of you,

00:20:49.190 --> 00:20:51.730
and it can manifest itself physically.

00:20:51.730 --> 00:20:55.540
So that's what led to me
having my first appointment

00:20:55.540 --> 00:20:57.950
in sometime in like late 2017.

00:20:57.950 --> 00:21:02.950
So it's crazy to think that
coming up on four years

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:04.240
of therapy.

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:07.810
I'm coming up on two years of therapy

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:10.710
in like the January, February timeframe.

00:21:10.710 --> 00:21:14.840
So that means I started like
just a couple of months,

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:18.010
had a couple of in-person
sessions before the pandemic hit,

00:21:18.010 --> 00:21:21.050
and we went to zoom meetings.

00:21:21.050 --> 00:21:26.050
And for me it's been
pretty much every week,

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:31.020
except when like scheduling challenges,

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:33.490
or our trips or whatever.

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:38.122
But yeah, the reason why I got into it,

00:21:38.122 --> 00:21:39.863
the best that I can remember is,

00:21:40.910 --> 00:21:45.910
yeah, Christy had had a few
therapists for many years

00:21:46.390 --> 00:21:51.390
and had been very open and
experienced the benefits of that,

00:21:53.450 --> 00:21:56.010
of going to therapy back
home in North Carolina,

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:58.830
and then out here when we moved out here,

00:21:58.830 --> 00:22:02.040
and like all the stresses

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:04.083
and everything associated with that.

00:22:05.870 --> 00:22:07.570
Therapy re-entered her life.

00:22:07.570 --> 00:22:12.300
And we always had a
really positive view of it

00:22:12.300 --> 00:22:15.220
because she had a very positive

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:16.960
and helpful experience with it.

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:18.620
And of course, we're talking to Jessie.

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:20.640
She's having positive experiences.

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:23.060
And then you get on board,

00:22:23.060 --> 00:22:26.143
and you start having positive experiences.

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:30.040
And I was always very open to it,

00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:33.463
but I think in a similar
way that you're describing,

00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:38.580
it was, well, I'll say
that I started going

00:22:38.580 --> 00:22:41.830
because Christy kept encouraging me,

00:22:41.830 --> 00:22:44.890
and I was benefiting from hearing

00:22:44.890 --> 00:22:47.410
from how everybody else is
benefiting from therapy.

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:49.940
So I felt like I was
getting therapy by proxy.

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:52.850
And I was, I've made jokes about that,

00:22:52.850 --> 00:22:56.143
but in a way to acknowledge that like,

00:22:57.230 --> 00:22:58.600
I'm warming up to this.

00:22:58.600 --> 00:22:59.910
I wasn't opposed to it,

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:02.830
but I didn't feel like
I had an urgent need

00:23:02.830 --> 00:23:04.210
to go to therapy,

00:23:04.210 --> 00:23:08.130
like to run there to
fix a specific problem.

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:12.740
I didn't think that I had any
problems that needed therapy.

00:23:12.740 --> 00:23:13.800
Meanwhile.
I was wrong.

00:23:13.800 --> 00:23:15.850
Meanwhile, I mean, we never,

00:23:15.850 --> 00:23:17.410
I don't know if we've
ever talked about this,

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:19.690
but meanwhile, like me, and Jesse,

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:22.780
and Christy would talk about,

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:25.172
well, when is Link gonna go to therapy?

00:23:25.172 --> 00:23:26.950
Yeah, and sometimes I will be there.

00:23:26.950 --> 00:23:27.783
But like, it's like-

00:23:27.783 --> 00:23:28.746
So, it wasn't weird.

00:23:28.746 --> 00:23:30.077
It wasn't like, when do we
intervene, I don't think.

00:23:30.077 --> 00:23:32.663
You can't really talk to someone.

00:23:33.690 --> 00:23:36.610
You had to let somebody come
to that decision on their own.

00:23:36.610 --> 00:23:38.500
I would say that for anybody who thinks

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:40.010
that somebody in your life needs to go,

00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:42.057
or you've experienced something,

00:23:42.057 --> 00:23:42.890
and you're like, ah, man,

00:23:42.890 --> 00:23:44.760
I wish that they could experience this.

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:45.660
Right.

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:48.009
You can't force them into it.

00:23:48.009 --> 00:23:49.970
Well, your spouse can kinda do that.

00:23:49.970 --> 00:23:50.803
Yeah.

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:53.760
Depending on the nature
of your relationship.

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:57.230
It became more of like,
I'm going to do this.

00:23:57.230 --> 00:23:59.160
I have plans to do this,

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:02.230
but they're not, it's not
pressing, and it's not imminent.

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:04.490
So I'm gonna just keep back burnering it

00:24:04.490 --> 00:24:07.540
for probably a couple of years.

00:24:07.540 --> 00:24:10.420
In the meantime, I would read books,

00:24:10.420 --> 00:24:12.750
a lot of the books that
Christy was reading

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:17.750
of the like introspective
self-help, self-discovery variety,

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:22.680
like stuff that she would be
recommended by her therapist

00:24:22.680 --> 00:24:24.530
or that she would take to therapy.

00:24:24.530 --> 00:24:26.240
So it was like an extension of that.

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:27.310
I would read those books,

00:24:27.310 --> 00:24:31.590
and I would start to feel
the benefits of that.

00:24:31.590 --> 00:24:35.600
And so I think the thing
that got me over the hump was

00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:40.020
I, as a result of my
spiritual deconstruction,

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:41.853
and my ongoing spiritual journey,

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:46.860
and really coming to
grips with my priority of,

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:50.150
in doing the work, as they say,

00:24:50.150 --> 00:24:53.550
like really invest in myself,
and understanding myself,

00:24:53.550 --> 00:24:55.140
discovering more,

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:59.860
and just, I knew there
were things in my past

00:24:59.860 --> 00:25:01.240
that affect me now,

00:25:01.240 --> 00:25:02.900
and I was fascinated by that,

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:05.220
and I knew I could benefit from it.

00:25:05.220 --> 00:25:07.323
So when I started going, it was like,

00:25:08.280 --> 00:25:12.270
I know I experienced a lot of anxiety,

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:14.563
and I do want help with that.

00:25:15.980 --> 00:25:19.650
There was like an opening meeting.

00:25:19.650 --> 00:25:22.550
The way that I found my
therapist was on like

00:25:22.550 --> 00:25:23.940
psychology.com.

00:25:23.940 --> 00:25:26.620
They have, you can search
for somebody locally,

00:25:26.620 --> 00:25:27.951
and you can read reviews.

00:25:27.951 --> 00:25:32.951
And there was an initial
interview process.

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:35.050
Which if you want to,

00:25:35.050 --> 00:25:36.780
you can tell how you met your therapist.

00:25:36.780 --> 00:25:38.610
But with mine it was like,

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:41.530
I didn't have a personal recommendation.

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:44.470
I just had an interview
meeting phone call.

00:25:44.470 --> 00:25:45.450
And I remember in that meeting,

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:47.300
I kinda just laid it all out.

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:50.651
I was like, I wanna send
a message that like,

00:25:50.651 --> 00:25:54.790
I'm an open book and I'm all in for

00:25:54.790 --> 00:25:57.120
however I can benefit from this.

00:25:57.120 --> 00:26:00.080
And I know that I deal with anxiety,

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:02.993
and I know that I have things in my past.

00:26:04.280 --> 00:26:06.000
When it came to the anxiety-

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:09.410
But it felt more like
an improvement thing,

00:26:09.410 --> 00:26:12.770
not a fixing thing, or not a
help, please help me thing,

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:15.620
was I think my mindset going in, honestly.

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:18.823
'Cause when it comes to
the anxiety element of it,

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:23.433
like, would you have considered yourself,

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:28.290
like what level of anxiety would you,

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:30.170
if somebody was like on
a scale of one to 10,

00:26:30.170 --> 00:26:32.460
how much anxiety do you have?

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:33.595
How would you have described that?

00:26:33.595 --> 00:26:37.517
I would say that I'm able
to manage it at like a six

00:26:37.517 --> 00:26:39.269
is what I would say at the time.

00:26:39.269 --> 00:26:40.790
And six, well-

00:26:40.790 --> 00:26:41.623
Maybe a five.

00:26:41.623 --> 00:26:43.030
Maybe I would have said five or six.

00:26:43.030 --> 00:26:45.002
I guess what I'm saying is-

00:26:45.002 --> 00:26:45.835
And then it spikes.

00:26:45.835 --> 00:26:48.940
Ultimately like if your
baseline level of anxiety

00:26:48.940 --> 00:26:51.130
is self-assessed at a six out of 10-

00:26:51.130 --> 00:26:51.963
Yeah.

00:26:51.963 --> 00:26:53.330
I'm no expert, but that's sort of like,

00:26:53.330 --> 00:26:54.320
okay, I'm actually-

00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:55.950
Well, because I dealt with that so long,

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:57.676
I was kinda, it just kinda-

00:26:57.676 --> 00:26:58.560
That's just, that's what everybody says.

00:26:58.560 --> 00:26:59.540
Feels like a normal.

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:03.410
But I remember going to the cardiologist

00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:06.310
back when Christy and
I were first married,

00:27:06.310 --> 00:27:09.380
and I worked as an engineer at IBM.

00:27:09.380 --> 00:27:11.710
And every day I'd come home
and lay down on the couch

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:13.690
and just to chill out.

00:27:13.690 --> 00:27:16.820
And then my heart would
just be like skipping beats

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:18.630
and like jumping out of my chest.

00:27:18.630 --> 00:27:20.550
Sometimes I would wake up
in the middle of the night

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:23.376
with like those heart
palpitations or whatever.

00:27:23.376 --> 00:27:25.290
I don't know if that's the official title,

00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:27.530
but like, and I talk.

00:27:27.530 --> 00:27:29.210
I remember I talked,

00:27:29.210 --> 00:27:31.610
I told my therapist very
early on about that.

00:27:31.610 --> 00:27:36.610
And I was like, that was 20 years ago.

00:27:36.620 --> 00:27:37.560
Yeah.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:39.800
But I knew that it was stress.

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:41.360
At least I really hoped that it was stress

00:27:41.360 --> 00:27:44.730
because when I sent in the
EKG readings, the tech said,

00:27:44.730 --> 00:27:47.720
yeah, this just looks
like you're stressed out,

00:27:47.720 --> 00:27:49.500
and then so I never
followed up with a doctor.

00:27:49.500 --> 00:27:51.490
And you're like, oh, of
course I can, handle that.

00:27:51.490 --> 00:27:53.230
But I kinda knew.

00:27:53.230 --> 00:27:54.480
And then I would,

00:27:54.480 --> 00:27:57.810
I developed various coping
mechanisms over the years

00:27:57.810 --> 00:28:00.330
and just kind of felt like
I was putting the anxiety

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:01.163
in its place.

00:28:01.163 --> 00:28:03.010
But there's also, I think
there's also a thing-

00:28:03.010 --> 00:28:06.480
So then only in certain
times would my heart rate,

00:28:06.480 --> 00:28:08.530
heart start to flutter like that

00:28:08.530 --> 00:28:11.230
when I was in a really stressful zone.

00:28:11.230 --> 00:28:14.350
I mean, I think, and I'm not
trying to generalize here,

00:28:14.350 --> 00:28:17.030
but I do think I am going to generalize.

00:28:17.030 --> 00:28:18.140
So I'm just, I guess I'm just giving

00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:20.463
a generalization disclaimer.

00:28:21.470 --> 00:28:25.560
That in my experience,
there are a lot of men who,

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:29.140
based on like certain cultural
pressures and expectations,

00:28:29.140 --> 00:28:32.270
especially if they are in
a relationship with a woman

00:28:32.270 --> 00:28:34.240
who is already kind of addressing

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:36.470
her mental and emotional health,

00:28:36.470 --> 00:28:38.360
I think there's a resistance.

00:28:38.360 --> 00:28:42.010
There's a resistance to
be like, I got, I feel,

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:45.520
oh, I mean, I gotta be the one
that's got it together, man.

00:28:45.520 --> 00:28:47.380
Like, and so even if you would-

00:28:47.380 --> 00:28:49.210
It may be that.
Assess yourself

00:28:49.210 --> 00:28:52.640
at a six out of 10 of
like a baseline anxiety-

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:53.556
I got it under control.

00:28:53.556 --> 00:28:54.650
You're like, I got it under control.

00:28:54.650 --> 00:28:57.060
Like I'm not about to explode.

00:28:57.060 --> 00:28:59.663
I'm a safe person.

00:29:00.850 --> 00:29:02.310
But, and so then you kinda say,

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:05.060
but she's really the one that needs help,

00:29:05.060 --> 00:29:06.290
and she's getting the help that she needs.

00:29:06.290 --> 00:29:07.340
Which is very-

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:08.490
And I would've never said that.

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:10.070
I'm just saying that there was a-

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:10.903
Subconscious.

00:29:10.903 --> 00:29:12.490
Subconscious thought that just,

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:13.830
I thought that that's just the way

00:29:13.830 --> 00:29:14.890
that it was supposed to work.

00:29:14.890 --> 00:29:17.120
I agree with that experience.

00:29:17.120 --> 00:29:18.440
I relate to that,

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:22.490
and I think that it
wasn't fair to our wives

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:24.290
to like put them in that box as like,

00:29:24.290 --> 00:29:27.383
okay, you're the ones that
need therapy 'cause you have.

00:29:28.550 --> 00:29:31.120
What I think was going on
was that they were actually

00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:36.120
much more in touch with what
was going on inside of them,

00:29:37.290 --> 00:29:41.230
and much more willing to seek help for it

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:43.760
when we were just out of touch with it.

00:29:43.760 --> 00:29:46.310
And I just kinda put it in this box,

00:29:46.310 --> 00:29:49.840
the physical evidence that
there's something going on

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:52.890
deep with inside of me
that I need to wrestle with

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:53.723
and deal with,

00:29:56.490 --> 00:29:58.020
and that's why it looked different.

00:29:58.020 --> 00:30:02.410
And I could say, well,
that's what I would describe.

00:30:02.410 --> 00:30:06.240
Well, Christy situation,

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:08.930
or she's the one who needs help,

00:30:08.930 --> 00:30:10.160
because that's what it looks like.

00:30:10.160 --> 00:30:13.150
But really what she was
demonstrating is an openness

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:16.100
and an in touchness with herself.

00:30:16.100 --> 00:30:17.490
And I didn't realize that, for me,

00:30:17.490 --> 00:30:21.210
it was a closed off, not
willing to go there thing.

00:30:21.210 --> 00:30:24.400
And I just chalked it up to differences.

00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:26.280
And I think that-
And needs.

00:30:26.280 --> 00:30:31.280
Unintentionally, there's
this amplification effect

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:34.620
that you actually make the,

00:30:34.620 --> 00:30:36.690
whatever it is that your partner

00:30:36.690 --> 00:30:41.490
or your friend is
dealing with more intense

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:43.440
by not addressing your own problems.

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:45.110
Right, so if you're somebody
who's walking around,

00:30:45.110 --> 00:30:47.540
I mean, obviously I got my own-

00:30:47.540 --> 00:30:49.010
You mean comparing, like-

00:30:49.010 --> 00:30:49.988
Well, no, no, I'm just saying.

00:30:49.988 --> 00:30:50.833
So-and-so's worse than me.

00:30:50.833 --> 00:30:54.740
If you're going around
a six out of 10 anxiety,

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:59.470
based level anxiety, you're
not the best person to be,

00:30:59.470 --> 00:31:02.930
to rely on for someone who's
going through those issues.

00:31:02.930 --> 00:31:06.290
You can even exacerbate the
anxiety that she's dealing with.

00:31:06.290 --> 00:31:09.060
I know that I did that for Jessie.

00:31:09.060 --> 00:31:12.400
As someone who wasn't
processing my emotions

00:31:12.400 --> 00:31:17.190
in a healthy way, I was
not the best resource.

00:31:17.190 --> 00:31:18.640
And again, I don't wanna get into like

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:20.810
codependent situation.

00:31:20.810 --> 00:31:24.050
What I'm saying is that
if you actually want to be

00:31:24.050 --> 00:31:26.100
the healthiest partner to your partner

00:31:26.100 --> 00:31:28.470
who is having their own issues,

00:31:28.470 --> 00:31:31.827
dealing with your own shit
is one way to address that.

00:31:31.827 --> 00:31:33.710
But when you're like,
I don't have anything,

00:31:33.710 --> 00:31:35.650
I don't have anything to deal with,

00:31:35.650 --> 00:31:37.560
then you can actually
create more of a burden

00:31:37.560 --> 00:31:40.400
for your partner who's already
dealing with their own stuff.

00:31:40.400 --> 00:31:45.400
So I went in with this like
optimization self-improvement,

00:31:46.010 --> 00:31:49.790
become my best self kind of thing.

00:31:49.790 --> 00:31:53.990
And I was willing to go to dark places

00:31:53.990 --> 00:31:55.280
in order to get there,

00:31:55.280 --> 00:31:59.200
but I was also like, how dark can it be?

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:00.033
Really?

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:01.863
And so,

00:32:06.580 --> 00:32:08.944
I did bring everything I had to it,

00:32:08.944 --> 00:32:11.070
but there's a little bit of a dance,

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:15.520
'cause you have to ease into
it because it's a relationship.

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:20.520
This is a complete stranger
with qualifications and reviews.

00:32:20.990 --> 00:32:23.500
So this dude's checked out,

00:32:23.500 --> 00:32:26.170
and I feel like we're starting
off on a good foundation,

00:32:26.170 --> 00:32:27.940
but you gotta build on that.

00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:29.507
And you gotta talk about yourself,

00:32:29.507 --> 00:32:31.960
and like you don't expect immediate.

00:32:31.960 --> 00:32:34.410
I'm learning that like, okay,

00:32:34.410 --> 00:32:38.670
I don't wanna expect
like immediate results.

00:32:38.670 --> 00:32:42.640
I gotta give this some
time and let this blossom.

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:44.810
Then the pandemic hit,

00:32:44.810 --> 00:32:47.853
and I lot of, I mean,

00:32:49.290 --> 00:32:53.170
therapy for me evolved
into crisis management,

00:32:53.170 --> 00:32:55.510
like the crisis of the pandemic

00:32:55.510 --> 00:32:58.930
and all the pressures that we then,

00:32:58.930 --> 00:33:01.580
that you mentioned earlier,
filtered through that.

00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:06.580
Leading a company and
being a resource for my,

00:33:07.160 --> 00:33:12.160
loving my family became
a week to week crisis.

00:33:12.206 --> 00:33:15.483
I've talked about Christy's
health challenges,

00:33:17.894 --> 00:33:22.450
and I'm so grateful that I
had gotten in under the wire,

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:24.900
and we had met in person
a few times, that then,

00:33:24.900 --> 00:33:29.900
and I had gotten a slot with
this guy so that I could then,

00:33:29.984 --> 00:33:32.310
when the really started hitting the fan,

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:33.890
and boy, was it hitting the fan

00:33:35.060 --> 00:33:37.530
for the majority of that year,

00:33:37.530 --> 00:33:42.490
every week was just help
me hold this together,

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:46.230
I think is what I brought to our meetings.

00:33:46.230 --> 00:33:48.110
Now this has happened.

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:49.210
How do I deal with this?

00:33:49.210 --> 00:33:51.820
How do I respond?

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:55.280
So it was kind of, it was
a survival type thing.

00:33:55.280 --> 00:33:59.640
And I've described the low
points and all those challenges.

00:33:59.640 --> 00:34:01.150
So I don't wanna go into that,

00:34:01.150 --> 00:34:06.150
but that really impacted the
tenor of what therapy became

00:34:08.930 --> 00:34:11.190
for me for that entire time.

00:34:11.190 --> 00:34:12.223
And then there was,

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:15.960
and then when things are better,

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:18.510
or like when the light shines,

00:34:18.510 --> 00:34:20.210
when the clouds clear for a little bit,

00:34:20.210 --> 00:34:21.980
and the light shines through,

00:34:21.980 --> 00:34:26.980
I remember there being a few sessions

00:34:27.010 --> 00:34:32.010
where it was actually about
kinda living in that moment of,

00:34:33.266 --> 00:34:37.660
I'd walked through all
of these difficult things

00:34:39.180 --> 00:34:44.180
and anticipated more,
but with this person,

00:34:44.530 --> 00:34:49.530
that then it was kind of
a, it was a sweet session

00:34:50.410 --> 00:34:54.040
when we could just kind of celebrate it.

00:34:54.040 --> 00:34:55.680
And so I discovered that like

00:34:55.680 --> 00:34:57.077
that was a different facet of therapy.

00:34:57.077 --> 00:34:59.410
Like that the relationship paid off,

00:34:59.410 --> 00:35:04.410
and it could be a positive
celebration of moments of growth

00:35:06.110 --> 00:35:07.973
or just of survival.

00:35:09.490 --> 00:35:14.450
But I'm painting a picture
of us not really getting back

00:35:14.450 --> 00:35:17.610
to that like deeper inner work

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:21.853
that was like the thing that I
thought I was signing up for.

00:35:23.690 --> 00:35:26.640
Because getting through the
day, and the next night,

00:35:26.640 --> 00:35:29.270
and the next week was kind of all that

00:35:29.270 --> 00:35:31.003
I had the capacity to focus on.

00:35:33.060 --> 00:35:38.060
So I would say that described
a phase of therapy for me.

00:35:38.910 --> 00:35:42.460
And I'm interested if you see
it that way or how you see it,

00:35:42.460 --> 00:35:44.170
but then I'll say there's a next phase

00:35:44.170 --> 00:35:45.770
that I'm kind of in now,

00:35:45.770 --> 00:35:48.440
which is really great news that like,

00:35:48.440 --> 00:35:52.050
there's been enough space between crises

00:35:52.050 --> 00:35:54.960
that I can start to say,

00:35:54.960 --> 00:35:57.320
what are some deeper
things that I wanna work on

00:35:57.320 --> 00:35:58.873
or places I wanna go?

00:36:00.210 --> 00:36:03.870
But it wasn't without a
very specific challenge

00:36:03.870 --> 00:36:06.850
that related to therapy
itself that I'll get into.

00:36:06.850 --> 00:36:08.810
But I wanted to hand it back over to you

00:36:08.810 --> 00:36:12.450
in terms of like how
you see the trajectory

00:36:12.450 --> 00:36:14.630
of building that relationship,

00:36:14.630 --> 00:36:16.300
and has it gone through phases?

00:36:16.300 --> 00:36:19.160
So my therapist was
Jessie's therapists first.

00:36:19.160 --> 00:36:19.993
Okay.

00:36:19.993 --> 00:36:24.463
And also my boys had been
to see him initially.

00:36:25.476 --> 00:36:29.100
Actually the thing that
was the impetus was

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:31.980
kinda recognizing that Shepherd had ADHD,

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:35.100
and that was, and somebody recommended

00:36:36.100 --> 00:36:38.820
who is now basically our
therapist that everyone can see.

00:36:38.820 --> 00:36:39.950
I mean, Jesse and I are the ones

00:36:39.950 --> 00:36:41.780
that see him regularly, but.

00:36:41.780 --> 00:36:44.080
So he had a lot of
insight into our family.

00:36:44.080 --> 00:36:47.490
And I think, and again, not
every therapist will do that.

00:36:47.490 --> 00:36:49.082
And it was a long process,

00:36:49.082 --> 00:36:50.610
and there's people have
different opinions about

00:36:50.610 --> 00:36:53.070
whether or not a therapist
should serve a couple

00:36:53.070 --> 00:36:54.453
or a family in that way.

00:36:55.330 --> 00:36:58.210
For us, it's a really good circumstance.

00:36:58.210 --> 00:37:00.360
I don't think I would
recommend it for everybody.

00:37:00.360 --> 00:37:02.820
But one of the things that did is

00:37:02.820 --> 00:37:05.490
there was a level of trust and connection.

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:07.730
And I think actually the
first time I met my therapist

00:37:07.730 --> 00:37:11.010
was in a couple session with Jesse there.

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:12.840
And that was the introduction to like,

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:16.090
oh, this is the guy that she's
been talking about for years,

00:37:16.090 --> 00:37:18.170
and now I know who he is.

00:37:18.170 --> 00:37:21.610
And so that first session
was like talking to somebody

00:37:21.610 --> 00:37:23.090
that I already knew.

00:37:23.090 --> 00:37:28.090
I think, when that's
the way that it starts,

00:37:28.530 --> 00:37:32.650
and of course, if your wife
has been going to a therapist,

00:37:32.650 --> 00:37:35.780
and your wife has been
talking about you in therapy-

00:37:35.780 --> 00:37:36.810
Yeah.

00:37:36.810 --> 00:37:40.510
You come as a, there's a
narrative attached to you.

00:37:40.510 --> 00:37:42.820
Right? Which could be a negative thing.

00:37:42.820 --> 00:37:45.510
But I think that Jesse was very fair

00:37:45.510 --> 00:37:47.700
in the way that she kind
of described the things

00:37:47.700 --> 00:37:49.353
that I dealt with.

00:37:50.450 --> 00:37:54.620
But then I immediately
confirmed all those things

00:37:54.620 --> 00:37:55.540
while meeting.

00:37:55.540 --> 00:37:56.967
And again, my main issue,

00:37:56.967 --> 00:38:01.967
the sort of the, the main
sort of ongoing thing

00:38:02.170 --> 00:38:06.120
that is my struggle is
the fact that I tend to

00:38:06.120 --> 00:38:09.190
intellectualize and
rationalize my emotions

00:38:09.190 --> 00:38:12.610
versus just experiencing them, right?

00:38:12.610 --> 00:38:16.593
So something comes in experientially,

00:38:17.490 --> 00:38:21.970
and there's a natural, emotional
response to that thing,

00:38:21.970 --> 00:38:26.117
whether it be sadness, or anger, whatever.

00:38:27.670 --> 00:38:30.300
And if it's an inconvenient emotion,

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:35.247
I think I've always seen
myself as a very self-reliant,

00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:38.580
super dependable.

00:38:38.580 --> 00:38:42.700
Like you can put me through
basically any circumstance,

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:45.440
and I'm gonna be the one
person that's still standing.

00:38:45.440 --> 00:38:46.459
Right?

00:38:46.459 --> 00:38:47.900
It's a pride thing.

00:38:47.900 --> 00:38:49.010
I don't know what it is,

00:38:49.010 --> 00:38:53.910
but I've, like give me
something else to do.

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:55.570
I will not break.

00:38:55.570 --> 00:38:58.770
Give me a new challenge.
I will succeed at it.

00:38:58.770 --> 00:39:03.770
And so negative emotions feel
like wastes of time for me.

00:39:04.750 --> 00:39:06.813
Right? Being sad about something.

00:39:08.330 --> 00:39:10.210
Well, that's like, who's got?

00:39:10.210 --> 00:39:13.540
Ain't nobody got time for
that. You know what I mean?

00:39:13.540 --> 00:39:15.090
And so what you end up doing is

00:39:15.090 --> 00:39:18.910
if there's something
that's genuinely difficult

00:39:18.910 --> 00:39:21.350
for me to deal with that should result in

00:39:21.350 --> 00:39:26.350
an outpouring of grief, or
sadness, frustration, whatever,

00:39:26.600 --> 00:39:29.890
I will immediately, and
I'm very skilled at this,

00:39:29.890 --> 00:39:33.850
refrain that circumstance to remind myself

00:39:33.850 --> 00:39:35.830
that it's actually not that bad.

00:39:35.830 --> 00:39:37.035
Like this is not that bad.

00:39:37.035 --> 00:39:39.050
Lots of people have it worse than you.

00:39:39.050 --> 00:39:41.720
Like you can actually
easily get to over here

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:42.900
and see the bright side of this,

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:44.160
and see the optimistic side,

00:39:44.160 --> 00:39:46.150
and see the future of
where this thing could go.

00:39:46.150 --> 00:39:47.930
And I immediately just move to the side,

00:39:47.930 --> 00:39:48.950
and I get on that track.

00:39:48.950 --> 00:39:52.400
And there's a lot of benefit in that

00:39:52.400 --> 00:39:54.940
when it comes to like being successful

00:39:54.940 --> 00:39:58.063
in capitalistic society, right?

00:39:59.166 --> 00:40:02.700
And so I have experienced a
tremendous amount of success

00:40:02.700 --> 00:40:03.670
in one sense

00:40:03.670 --> 00:40:06.760
because of my tendency to
not let myself feel things

00:40:06.760 --> 00:40:07.640
and just to kind of.

00:40:07.640 --> 00:40:09.840
But what was happening over time is that

00:40:09.840 --> 00:40:12.680
if you continue to do that,

00:40:12.680 --> 00:40:15.100
those emotions are going
somewhere to be processed.

00:40:15.100 --> 00:40:18.930
Usually if it's a sadness
that you're dealing with,

00:40:18.930 --> 00:40:21.210
then it will be an outburst of anger.

00:40:21.210 --> 00:40:25.653
It will be a short temper, which
I definitely have at times.

00:40:27.271 --> 00:40:30.900
And it will be like, oh,
you went from zero to 100.

00:40:30.900 --> 00:40:32.880
Like where did that come from?

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:34.200
The kids came in and did something,

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:35.880
and you snapped at him immediately.

00:40:35.880 --> 00:40:37.270
Like what was?

00:40:37.270 --> 00:40:40.640
A lot of that comes from not processing

00:40:40.640 --> 00:40:41.820
and letting myself feel things.

00:40:41.820 --> 00:40:43.950
So that's my main issue.

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:45.670
There's a lot of other issues,

00:40:45.670 --> 00:40:48.320
but I'm saying that that's
the main sort of emotional

00:40:49.810 --> 00:40:53.560
sort of misfire that
happens repeatedly with me.

00:40:53.560 --> 00:40:54.499
And so-

00:40:54.499 --> 00:40:56.290
And that's something that you discovered.

00:40:56.290 --> 00:40:58.262
You became acquainted with it in therapy.

00:40:58.262 --> 00:40:59.300
'Cause what I would have told you,

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:01.090
and what I would have told Jesse

00:41:01.090 --> 00:41:02.320
is I would have just told you

00:41:02.320 --> 00:41:04.310
the positive side of that thing.

00:41:04.310 --> 00:41:06.610
I would be like, I'm kind of a stoic guy.

00:41:06.610 --> 00:41:10.390
Like I can get very
enthusiastic about things,

00:41:10.390 --> 00:41:11.860
and it's not like I don't cry.

00:41:11.860 --> 00:41:14.900
Like I said, I'll cry
at Hallmark commercials.

00:41:14.900 --> 00:41:18.240
But, and then when my
therapist explained to me,

00:41:18.240 --> 00:41:20.860
he was like, well, the reason you do that

00:41:20.860 --> 00:41:23.650
is because you're very
willing to feel emotions

00:41:23.650 --> 00:41:25.193
for other people.

00:41:26.290 --> 00:41:28.270
Like if I go to a wedding,

00:41:28.270 --> 00:41:30.810
I don't care if I know
the people, I'm gonna cry.

00:41:30.810 --> 00:41:34.233
If I go to a funeral,
I'll most likely cry.

00:41:35.540 --> 00:41:39.970
But if I am experiencing
something, very rarely will I cry.

00:41:39.970 --> 00:41:41.360
If it's something that's an emotion

00:41:41.360 --> 00:41:43.270
that I'm the target of it,

00:41:43.270 --> 00:41:45.680
and I'm the one who's
supposed to feel sadness,

00:41:45.680 --> 00:41:47.250
I'll deflect it, and I'll save it.

00:41:47.250 --> 00:41:48.940
You put it over here in a little jar.

00:41:48.940 --> 00:41:50.710
It's building up.

00:41:50.710 --> 00:41:52.890
That jar has gotta be emptied somehow.

00:41:52.890 --> 00:41:54.190
It's either gonna come out as anger,

00:41:54.190 --> 00:41:57.130
or it's gonna come out as
emotion for someone else.

00:41:57.130 --> 00:41:58.813
I just always was like,

00:41:58.813 --> 00:42:01.110
I never, had never sorted
through any of this stuff.

00:42:01.110 --> 00:42:03.460
I was just like, I'm like my dad.

00:42:03.460 --> 00:42:05.630
I'm just like, I'm super dependable.

00:42:05.630 --> 00:42:07.930
I'm not the emotional person in the room.

00:42:07.930 --> 00:42:11.290
Like, I'm not gonna
take any of your energy.

00:42:11.290 --> 00:42:13.400
Like if you're in a relationship with me,

00:42:13.400 --> 00:42:15.460
if you're working with me,

00:42:15.460 --> 00:42:18.460
we're never going to have
to stop to accommodate me.

00:42:18.460 --> 00:42:20.300
Like I'm not that guy.

00:42:20.300 --> 00:42:23.278
And so I just was like, and
that's what makes me great.

00:42:23.278 --> 00:42:24.980
You know what I'm saying?

00:42:24.980 --> 00:42:27.680
But what my therapist was beginning to,

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:29.930
well, and Jesse was
saying things about it.

00:42:29.930 --> 00:42:31.030
Like, well, you just,

00:42:31.030 --> 00:42:33.330
you're not just letting
yourself feel this.

00:42:33.330 --> 00:42:35.480
You're not opening yourself up to this.

00:42:35.480 --> 00:42:38.540
It was just a couple of
meetings as I explained.

00:42:38.540 --> 00:42:40.330
He was like, tell me about yourself.

00:42:40.330 --> 00:42:41.380
I mean, the first few meetings

00:42:41.380 --> 00:42:43.030
were talking about my upbringing,

00:42:44.020 --> 00:42:45.780
talking about the way I see things.

00:42:45.780 --> 00:42:49.010
And I'm presenting
myself in a certain way,

00:42:49.010 --> 00:42:51.050
and I wasn't trying to present
a good picture of myself.

00:42:51.050 --> 00:42:52.850
I was like, I know I need something

00:42:52.850 --> 00:42:55.090
because I mean, I've got all
this physical stuff happening.

00:42:55.090 --> 00:42:57.450
I don't really understand what it is

00:42:57.450 --> 00:43:01.820
'cause I don't have the
language to talk about this.

00:43:01.820 --> 00:43:06.820
But, and my therapist is a
very good friend now as well.

00:43:06.870 --> 00:43:07.703
Right?

00:43:07.703 --> 00:43:11.070
So he can say, he just
tells me like it is.

00:43:11.070 --> 00:43:11.903
And sometimes-

00:43:11.903 --> 00:43:13.380
Hold on, let's not skip ahead though.

00:43:13.380 --> 00:43:16.470
So, but early on you were
telling about yourself,

00:43:16.470 --> 00:43:19.270
and then you were unpacking these things,

00:43:19.270 --> 00:43:21.760
and he was helping you
come to these realizations

00:43:21.760 --> 00:43:23.210
that you've just shared.

00:43:23.210 --> 00:43:25.850
Yeah, but like, what I'm getting at is,

00:43:25.850 --> 00:43:28.390
there was an immediate
relational connection.

00:43:28.390 --> 00:43:29.640
So I'm not just saying he's a friend now.

00:43:29.640 --> 00:43:32.690
There's an immediate sort
of relational connection,

00:43:32.690 --> 00:43:36.200
which is not necessarily
normal or expected.

00:43:36.200 --> 00:43:39.133
I will just say that. It
just worked out that way.

00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:42.720
And I don't even think it's
necessary, to be clear,

00:43:42.720 --> 00:43:45.510
but in my situation, it was great.

00:43:45.510 --> 00:43:48.210
But like, I specifically remember a time

00:43:48.210 --> 00:43:50.622
where I would like explain
something, and he just laughed.

00:43:50.622 --> 00:43:52.352
You know what I'm saying?

00:43:52.352 --> 00:43:53.185
Yeah.

00:43:53.185 --> 00:43:57.340
Because he was observing how adept I was

00:43:57.340 --> 00:44:00.070
at moving myself out away from like,

00:44:00.070 --> 00:44:02.440
you get on the uncomfortable
peak of an emotion,

00:44:02.440 --> 00:44:04.353
and you immediately dodge it.

00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:06.860
And he could just observe that happening,

00:44:06.860 --> 00:44:08.310
and he would be like, no, no, no, stop.

00:44:08.310 --> 00:44:12.160
Let's stay there with this thing.

00:44:12.160 --> 00:44:15.300
Where do you feel it?
Like where physically?

00:44:15.300 --> 00:44:16.940
And like the first time he asked me

00:44:16.940 --> 00:44:20.330
where I felt something
physically, I laughed at him.

00:44:20.330 --> 00:44:23.860
Because I was just like, man,
what does that even mean?

00:44:23.860 --> 00:44:25.250
Where do I feel it?

00:44:25.250 --> 00:44:26.880
Like I can't even tell you what I feel,

00:44:26.880 --> 00:44:28.220
much less where I feel it.

00:44:28.220 --> 00:44:29.053
Yeah.

00:44:29.053 --> 00:44:31.210
But I've since, we'll get into it later

00:44:31.210 --> 00:44:33.860
like what I've actually learned
and how I've applied that,

00:44:33.860 --> 00:44:36.860
but that was kinda my
initial experience was like,

00:44:36.860 --> 00:44:41.860
immediately kinda understanding
what the challenge was

00:44:42.090 --> 00:44:44.853
and having it explained to me in a.

00:44:47.315 --> 00:44:49.800
And this is my tendency is I'm gonna like

00:44:49.800 --> 00:44:52.130
try to like academically
define what it was.

00:44:52.130 --> 00:44:53.710
And so right from the
beginning, I was like,

00:44:53.710 --> 00:44:56.260
oh, this is what's wrong with me.

00:44:56.260 --> 00:44:59.430
So this is the thing that
I need to start fixing.

00:44:59.430 --> 00:45:00.880
That happened really quickly.

00:45:02.060 --> 00:45:03.021
The fixing-

00:45:03.021 --> 00:45:03.854
Then what?

00:45:03.854 --> 00:45:06.923
The fixing is a different process.

00:45:08.570 --> 00:45:10.280
Well, so I do a session,

00:45:10.280 --> 00:45:14.370
I've consistently done every
other week, not weekly.

00:45:14.370 --> 00:45:16.703
For an hour and a half instead of an hour.

00:45:17.670 --> 00:45:21.940
Well, yeah, like a double session.

00:45:21.940 --> 00:45:23.990
So I guess it ends up being
the same amount of time

00:45:23.990 --> 00:45:25.940
as if I was doing it every single week.

00:45:27.870 --> 00:45:32.810
But, and it's actually gotten
a little bit shorter recently,

00:45:32.810 --> 00:45:33.683
in the past year or so.

00:45:33.683 --> 00:45:36.660
It's pretty much just a single
session every other week.

00:45:36.660 --> 00:45:39.460
Did it, when I described
like the crisis thing,

00:45:39.460 --> 00:45:42.790
like, I mean, are there
times when you're like,

00:45:42.790 --> 00:45:45.740
this happened or there's a crisis?

00:45:45.740 --> 00:45:48.640
Yeah, well, every session-

00:45:48.640 --> 00:45:50.240
And it's external.

00:45:50.240 --> 00:45:51.920
It's an external crisis.

00:45:51.920 --> 00:45:52.800
Well, yeah, usually-

00:45:52.800 --> 00:45:54.370
Obviously there's an internal component.

00:45:54.370 --> 00:45:56.135
It usually starts with that, yeah.

00:45:56.135 --> 00:45:59.363
Yeah, I, and I still do this.

00:45:59.363 --> 00:46:00.900
It's still my tendency,

00:46:00.900 --> 00:46:05.883
but I will start off a
session kinda telling him

00:46:06.970 --> 00:46:11.640
everything that's kinda going
on in no particular order.

00:46:11.640 --> 00:46:13.060
I don't take, I don't have an agenda.

00:46:13.060 --> 00:46:17.560
I don't really think about it
until I see him on the call.

00:46:17.560 --> 00:46:19.800
And then I'm just like,
I just start going,

00:46:19.800 --> 00:46:21.260
and I just start talking.

00:46:21.260 --> 00:46:26.260
And the things that are the heaviest

00:46:27.010 --> 00:46:31.570
and feel like the biggest sort
of burden are the things that

00:46:31.570 --> 00:46:34.233
become very obvious
very quickly as I talk.

00:46:35.310 --> 00:46:37.980
And then he'll kind of rewind.

00:46:37.980 --> 00:46:40.440
Let's go back to this,

00:46:40.440 --> 00:46:42.900
and then we'll kinda talk about that.

00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:46.250
And again, it's a subtle process

00:46:46.250 --> 00:46:48.350
of just getting me to actually

00:46:48.350 --> 00:46:52.110
understand what it is that I'm feeling.

00:46:52.110 --> 00:46:56.230
It's pretty simple with me
because the issue is so severe.

00:46:56.230 --> 00:46:57.120
You know what I'm saying?

00:46:57.120 --> 00:47:00.810
I'm sure there's other things
to continue to deal with,

00:47:00.810 --> 00:47:04.010
but because the tendency
is to just reframe,

00:47:04.010 --> 00:47:09.010
redefine, and cope versus
feel, it's really just like,

00:47:09.370 --> 00:47:10.780
all right, let's talk about that.

00:47:10.780 --> 00:47:12.270
Let's see what you're feeling.

00:47:12.270 --> 00:47:15.120
And so one of the things that,

00:47:15.120 --> 00:47:17.220
I know we're gonna talk a little bit about

00:47:18.890 --> 00:47:20.993
how you gauge your own progress.

00:47:21.830 --> 00:47:22.990
So I don't wanna say that now,

00:47:22.990 --> 00:47:27.350
but because the issue is
kinda singular for me,

00:47:27.350 --> 00:47:30.160
and yes, it will be like,
we're having particular issue.

00:47:30.160 --> 00:47:33.880
And again, 'cause he
knows our family so well,

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:36.110
he might be like, well, I already know

00:47:36.110 --> 00:47:38.100
the issue that so-and-so is dealing with.

00:47:38.100 --> 00:47:39.750
Now, he doesn't tell me anything.

00:47:40.860 --> 00:47:43.200
There's privacy. It's
a little complicated.

00:47:43.200 --> 00:47:44.410
But like he, if I had to say,

00:47:44.410 --> 00:47:46.090
well, Jessie's going through
this thing right now,

00:47:46.090 --> 00:47:47.570
or Locke's going through
this thing right now,

00:47:47.570 --> 00:47:49.140
he already has insight into that

00:47:49.140 --> 00:47:51.870
'cause he's already
talked to them about it.

00:47:51.870 --> 00:47:55.010
Which again, for me,
has been a great benefit

00:47:55.010 --> 00:47:57.340
because you got somebody
who understands the dynamics

00:47:57.340 --> 00:47:58.770
on both sides of the situation.

00:47:58.770 --> 00:48:01.750
So there are times when
you're walking through

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:06.130
like processing a crisis,

00:48:06.130 --> 00:48:11.130
but the general mode for
you is like a continual,

00:48:12.660 --> 00:48:17.660
an exercise of bringing
your emotions to the table

00:48:19.260 --> 00:48:23.220
and processing things on
a level, on that level,

00:48:23.220 --> 00:48:27.830
like instead of just
the way that you would

00:48:27.830 --> 00:48:32.610
left to your own devices pre-therapy.

00:48:32.610 --> 00:48:34.980
Yeah, it's just kind of
sorting through all that stuff.

00:48:34.980 --> 00:48:35.813
Okay.

00:48:35.813 --> 00:48:38.210
Because, it's interesting
because there's a difference

00:48:38.210 --> 00:48:42.270
between vulnerability and
emotional vulnerability.

00:48:42.270 --> 00:48:46.740
I'm actually a really vulnerable
person in terms of like,

00:48:46.740 --> 00:48:49.270
I'm not a secretive, protective person.

00:48:49.270 --> 00:48:50.760
If I meet you on the subway,

00:48:50.760 --> 00:48:53.460
which I don't go on the subway
'cause I'm in Los Angeles.

00:48:53.460 --> 00:48:55.530
We call it a Metro, and I
don't usually take that.

00:48:55.530 --> 00:48:59.283
But I could have a
conversation with somebody,

00:49:00.250 --> 00:49:03.230
and immediately get into
like some deep stuff

00:49:03.230 --> 00:49:08.220
and be very personally
disclosing about myself,

00:49:08.220 --> 00:49:09.560
my issues, et cetera.

00:49:09.560 --> 00:49:11.710
Yeah, you can share things.

00:49:11.710 --> 00:49:12.960
Yeah.
Factually.

00:49:12.960 --> 00:49:16.350
And I'm a very, I'm a
very introspective person.

00:49:16.350 --> 00:49:19.960
Meaning that I'm willing
to look at myself,

00:49:19.960 --> 00:49:22.870
and analyze my actions,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:49:22.870 --> 00:49:27.870
But those are not substitutes
for emotional vulnerability.

00:49:28.820 --> 00:49:33.257
Again, yes, I've cried on
this podcast, whatever.

00:49:36.270 --> 00:49:38.830
But what I'm talking about is just like

00:49:40.050 --> 00:49:45.050
getting to a place where
I give myself permission

00:49:45.400 --> 00:49:46.750
to feel something.

00:49:46.750 --> 00:49:49.320
Like it's okay, you
can stop and feel this.

00:49:49.320 --> 00:49:53.940
Like no task is gonna be left aside.

00:49:53.940 --> 00:49:58.000
Or it's okay for this person
to see you in this state.

00:49:58.000 --> 00:50:02.130
So I wanna, 'cause those
issues can get conflated

00:50:02.130 --> 00:50:02.963
pretty easily.

00:50:02.963 --> 00:50:03.796
Well, as an aside,

00:50:03.796 --> 00:50:05.680
I think we're both very vulnerable here,

00:50:05.680 --> 00:50:07.410
but there are things that we've decided

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:10.780
that we're comfortable sharing,

00:50:10.780 --> 00:50:14.840
but there's moments when
we might be experiencing

00:50:15.780 --> 00:50:17.380
something on an emotional level.

00:50:17.380 --> 00:50:21.010
Like if we tear up or
cry about something here,

00:50:21.010 --> 00:50:22.510
that happens much more rarely.

00:50:22.510 --> 00:50:24.570
It's a different type of vulnerability.

00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:25.710
So even if you look at it here.

00:50:25.710 --> 00:50:26.543
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:50:26.543 --> 00:50:30.230
See, the reason why I'm asking
like the questions of like,

00:50:30.230 --> 00:50:32.640
how you go about therapy
and how you view it,

00:50:32.640 --> 00:50:34.410
is because I feel like,

00:50:34.410 --> 00:50:37.440
even though it's been almost two years

00:50:37.440 --> 00:50:40.390
for me, I'm still just getting started

00:50:40.390 --> 00:50:45.390
because there was so much
acute crisis to deal with.

00:50:45.950 --> 00:50:48.790
And there was definitely,

00:50:48.790 --> 00:50:52.630
I mean, I'm not saying that
there weren't times when like

00:50:52.630 --> 00:50:56.660
we've made connections between my past

00:50:56.660 --> 00:50:57.950
and how that impacts me now,

00:50:57.950 --> 00:51:00.370
or what's going on inside of me.

00:51:00.370 --> 00:51:04.810
And definitely I've been
processing the emotional aspects

00:51:04.810 --> 00:51:07.980
of the crisis that I'm going through

00:51:07.980 --> 00:51:11.239
and finding it as a resource.

00:51:11.239 --> 00:51:15.560
I do tend to overanalyze everything.

00:51:15.560 --> 00:51:19.960
And so at certain points I
would describe therapy as,

00:51:19.960 --> 00:51:23.970
you know what I feel like if
I were just talking to a wall,

00:51:23.970 --> 00:51:26.800
and I was disciplined enough
to like verbally process

00:51:26.800 --> 00:51:29.470
to that wall every week for an hour.

00:51:29.470 --> 00:51:30.520
And then kind of,

00:51:30.520 --> 00:51:33.750
that I would come to a lot
of the same conclusions.

00:51:33.750 --> 00:51:36.556
Like that's probably not true.

00:51:36.556 --> 00:51:37.643
That says a lot about your therapist.

00:51:38.989 --> 00:51:39.930
I'm just kidding.

00:51:39.930 --> 00:51:41.481
It says a lot about me, right?

00:51:41.481 --> 00:51:42.550
I understand exactly.

00:51:42.550 --> 00:51:43.550
I mean, I'm just being honest

00:51:43.550 --> 00:51:44.800
that sometimes it feels that way

00:51:44.800 --> 00:51:47.273
'cause it's like, well,
how much did he say?

00:51:48.240 --> 00:51:49.300
I can talk a lot.

00:51:49.300 --> 00:51:51.240
I can get, you know I can get going,

00:51:51.240 --> 00:51:53.190
and I am such a verbal processor,

00:51:53.190 --> 00:51:56.800
that like hearing myself is a huge part

00:51:56.800 --> 00:51:58.760
of the process for me.

00:51:58.760 --> 00:52:01.220
And on the skeptical side, I'm like,

00:52:01.220 --> 00:52:04.500
yeah, he only asked me
three questions, but-

00:52:04.500 --> 00:52:05.960
How much am I paying him?

00:52:05.960 --> 00:52:07.140
And how much am I paying him?

00:52:07.140 --> 00:52:09.300
But on the other side it's,

00:52:09.300 --> 00:52:11.003
those are some pivotal questions.

00:52:13.010 --> 00:52:14.970
And those moments when
I stopped, and thought,

00:52:14.970 --> 00:52:16.420
and answered to the question,

00:52:17.340 --> 00:52:19.440
probably redirected the whole conversation

00:52:19.440 --> 00:52:21.670
in a way that the wall wouldn't.

00:52:21.670 --> 00:52:23.240
But I'm just being honest and saying-

00:52:23.240 --> 00:52:24.600
If it's a Tesla wall, I don't know.

00:52:24.600 --> 00:52:26.610
I always like feel that way.

00:52:26.610 --> 00:52:30.120
But I've noticed in the points

00:52:30.120 --> 00:52:33.470
when there's not something
that like I feel like I am,

00:52:33.470 --> 00:52:36.740
need to run to someone for help,

00:52:36.740 --> 00:52:38.920
that I'm drowning in some way,

00:52:38.920 --> 00:52:41.780
or that I want to help
somebody else that I love,

00:52:41.780 --> 00:52:42.613
or something.

00:52:42.613 --> 00:52:47.500
If there's not an emergency
and the session's coming up,

00:52:48.690 --> 00:52:53.690
I just really started noticing
the amount of trepidation

00:52:53.920 --> 00:52:55.760
that I would bring to it.

00:52:55.760 --> 00:52:59.370
And this started happening,
I think, pretty early.

00:52:59.370 --> 00:53:02.940
Like I actually, I went
through a period of time

00:53:02.940 --> 00:53:07.880
in the first year where I
would jot down on my phone,

00:53:07.880 --> 00:53:11.540
like a sentence or two
from the therapy session.

00:53:11.540 --> 00:53:12.740
And I went back and looked,

00:53:12.740 --> 00:53:15.923
and I realized that like a
year ago this was happening.

00:53:17.080 --> 00:53:21.237
Like we had gone through a lot of things.

00:53:21.237 --> 00:53:22.660
And like, it was one of those,

00:53:22.660 --> 00:53:25.040
like the clouds had cleared moment.

00:53:25.040 --> 00:53:28.690
And I jotted down that I was just,

00:53:28.690 --> 00:53:31.390
I approached the sessions
with so much trepidation

00:53:31.390 --> 00:53:35.283
because I did not know what
I wanted to talk about.

00:53:36.300 --> 00:53:38.520
And then I noticed over the past few,

00:53:38.520 --> 00:53:40.070
and by the way, when that happened,

00:53:40.070 --> 00:53:43.120
I remember I canceled an appointment.

00:53:43.120 --> 00:53:47.450
And then it was, and then
the next week I like,

00:53:47.450 --> 00:53:49.173
I wanted to cancel again.

00:53:50.260 --> 00:53:54.980
And so, I ended up talking
about it a little bit

00:53:54.980 --> 00:53:56.330
instead of canceling the.

00:53:56.330 --> 00:53:58.210
I like, I felt like I confessed it.

00:53:58.210 --> 00:54:00.440
I was like, listen, I
canceled on you last week

00:54:00.440 --> 00:54:03.440
because I was just too
afraid to show up and not.

00:54:03.440 --> 00:54:05.940
And the unknown just was too much for me.

00:54:05.940 --> 00:54:08.800
I didn't know what I wanted to talk about.

00:54:08.800 --> 00:54:12.920
And then a lot of shit hit
the fan again, or I guess,

00:54:12.920 --> 00:54:16.160
because over the last few weeks,

00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:18.870
maybe months, it's come up again.

00:54:18.870 --> 00:54:22.340
And I guess it's a good sign
that like, I have the room.

00:54:22.340 --> 00:54:27.070
I have the head space to
say, I'm not drowning here.

00:54:27.070 --> 00:54:27.903
I'm not afraid.

00:54:27.903 --> 00:54:31.800
I'm not desperate for help
about a specific thing.

00:54:31.800 --> 00:54:34.029
But my mind immediately goes to, now what?

00:54:34.029 --> 00:54:36.380
That's how I know therapy.

00:54:36.380 --> 00:54:39.850
So going back to that first place of like,

00:54:39.850 --> 00:54:43.050
I'm gonna just start working
on some like longer term,

00:54:43.050 --> 00:54:46.920
or deeper issues, shadow work type stuff,

00:54:46.920 --> 00:54:50.563
the real sexy, hard stuff.

00:54:51.570 --> 00:54:54.723
Instead, my mind, I would
just get frozen with,

00:54:56.450 --> 00:54:59.040
I don't know what to say.

00:54:59.040 --> 00:55:01.400
And I would get so stressed out

00:55:01.400 --> 00:55:06.250
like five, six days in advance.

00:55:06.250 --> 00:55:08.770
And I would notice the moment

00:55:08.770 --> 00:55:11.453
that I would come up with
something, I'd feel relief.

00:55:12.320 --> 00:55:14.930
And I would, I'd realize that like,

00:55:14.930 --> 00:55:16.240
this is what I felt in college

00:55:16.240 --> 00:55:17.720
every time I had an assignment.

00:55:17.720 --> 00:55:19.510
Until I could finish the assignment

00:55:19.510 --> 00:55:22.260
or develop a plan that I
knew I could finish it,

00:55:22.260 --> 00:55:24.490
it would drive me nuts.

00:55:24.490 --> 00:55:27.680
And I started to think,
this might be a good thing

00:55:27.680 --> 00:55:29.639
to talk about in therapy.

00:55:29.639 --> 00:55:31.026
Ding, ding, ding, ding, ding.

00:55:31.026 --> 00:55:32.360
But you know-

00:55:32.360 --> 00:55:33.780
But, so my question is,

00:55:33.780 --> 00:55:37.560
because again, like what
I'm hearing, as your friend,

00:55:37.560 --> 00:55:39.130
as you say these things,

00:55:39.130 --> 00:55:41.320
is just like, yes,

00:55:41.320 --> 00:55:43.788
the reason that you should be in therapy

00:55:43.788 --> 00:55:46.830
is the, it being evidenced

00:55:46.830 --> 00:55:48.570
in the way that you approach therapy.

00:55:48.570 --> 00:55:49.403
Yeah.

00:55:49.403 --> 00:55:53.300
And so when you take
this to your therapist,

00:55:53.300 --> 00:55:54.670
what do you feel?

00:55:54.670 --> 00:55:58.210
Do you feel like there are some?

00:55:58.210 --> 00:56:00.460
And again, I understand
it's not about necessarily.

00:56:00.460 --> 00:56:03.920
You're not necessarily paying
them for their insights,

00:56:03.920 --> 00:56:05.370
so to speak.

00:56:05.370 --> 00:56:08.140
It is like paying for time to process,

00:56:08.140 --> 00:56:09.080
which everybody needs,

00:56:09.080 --> 00:56:11.920
but they are trained professionals.

00:56:11.920 --> 00:56:16.630
So do you feel like when you take that-

00:56:16.630 --> 00:56:19.423
A year ago when I took it to him,

00:56:21.810 --> 00:56:23.750
he was like, you know what,

00:56:23.750 --> 00:56:27.790
I want you to feel like you can come with

00:56:27.790 --> 00:56:31.600
absolutely blank slate,
not knowing anything.

00:56:31.600 --> 00:56:33.530
Some of the best sessions
I have with people

00:56:33.530 --> 00:56:36.690
are when they have no clue
what we're going to talk about.

00:56:36.690 --> 00:56:39.230
And they still show up.

00:56:39.230 --> 00:56:41.840
And he encouraged me to think about it as

00:56:41.840 --> 00:56:46.620
I'm prioritizing myself
and something that I need.

00:56:46.620 --> 00:56:48.763
This is me time that's protected.

00:56:51.296 --> 00:56:56.296
And he didn't say, you're
not performing for me,

00:56:56.720 --> 00:57:01.263
or we didn't really, he just
reassured me at the time.

00:57:01.263 --> 00:57:02.240
It was like, you know what?

00:57:02.240 --> 00:57:04.790
You don't have to come up with anything.

00:57:04.790 --> 00:57:05.803
I got this.

00:57:06.790 --> 00:57:08.140
I can handle this.

00:57:08.140 --> 00:57:12.363
And I could not bring myself
to believe him in action.

00:57:13.650 --> 00:57:14.550
It was difficult.

00:57:14.550 --> 00:57:17.300
And then more shit hit the fan,

00:57:17.300 --> 00:57:20.403
and I had stuff that I
felt like was urgent,

00:57:21.320 --> 00:57:22.470
and it was.

00:57:22.470 --> 00:57:25.320
So, and then it kinda, again,
it moved to the back burner,

00:57:25.320 --> 00:57:26.870
but I realized that was a year ago.

00:57:26.870 --> 00:57:30.340
That was October that I
put that in my journal,

00:57:32.850 --> 00:57:33.793
my phone journal.

00:57:35.110 --> 00:57:37.850
So a few weeks ago, a few sessions ago,

00:57:37.850 --> 00:57:39.023
I brought it up again.

00:57:39.940 --> 00:57:42.410
And I was like, this is still happening.

00:57:42.410 --> 00:57:44.740
I'm having such a difficult time.

00:57:44.740 --> 00:57:47.060
I don't know what my hangup is.

00:57:47.060 --> 00:57:48.690
We started to explore like,

00:57:48.690 --> 00:57:53.663
well, I wanna make sure it's
worth my time and my money.

00:57:54.830 --> 00:57:59.410
I wanna, am I afraid that
I'm not demonstrating,

00:58:00.620 --> 00:58:02.320
that I'm not making the most of this,

00:58:02.320 --> 00:58:03.850
that I'm not bringing enough to this,

00:58:03.850 --> 00:58:06.483
and my therapist knows it?

00:58:07.336 --> 00:58:09.380
And I'm telling him
this stuff at that time.

00:58:09.380 --> 00:58:13.450
And I'm realizing that
there's definitely barriers

00:58:13.450 --> 00:58:18.450
there for me to accepting
that as a place that is,

00:58:19.580 --> 00:58:22.010
that's a nonjudgmental zone.

00:58:22.010 --> 00:58:24.000
Because there's a lot of things going on,

00:58:24.000 --> 00:58:26.750
but one of the things
that I realized was that

00:58:26.750 --> 00:58:29.260
I just assume that he is analyzing me

00:58:29.260 --> 00:58:34.260
in the same way that I'm
analyzing the session and myself.

00:58:34.330 --> 00:58:38.870
And control is obviously
a big thing for me

00:58:38.870 --> 00:58:40.160
that I'm working on,

00:58:40.160 --> 00:58:41.310
and I see it at play here.

00:58:41.310 --> 00:58:44.810
It's like, when I don't feel
like I can, I have plans,

00:58:44.810 --> 00:58:48.610
meaning that I have control
over where the therapy starts,

00:58:48.610 --> 00:58:53.610
so then I know when I've gotten to it,

00:58:54.060 --> 00:58:55.388
when I've gotten to the answer,

00:58:55.388 --> 00:58:58.150
when I've made the most of the session,

00:58:58.150 --> 00:59:00.830
when I've gotten my
money and time's worth,

00:59:00.830 --> 00:59:03.650
when I've gotten, when I've demonstrated

00:59:03.650 --> 00:59:04.950
that I'm willing to make progress

00:59:04.950 --> 00:59:07.010
because I actually have made progress.

00:59:07.010 --> 00:59:08.690
'Cause I brought a starting point,

00:59:08.690 --> 00:59:10.610
and then there was some
sort of conclusion.

00:59:10.610 --> 00:59:14.020
I definitely found myself
towards the end of a session.

00:59:14.020 --> 00:59:16.060
I would look at the clock
throughout the session

00:59:16.060 --> 00:59:18.500
and realize, okay, I'm halfway through.

00:59:18.500 --> 00:59:20.150
There's something else
I wanted to talk about

00:59:20.150 --> 00:59:21.160
so I'm gonna get to that now.

00:59:21.160 --> 00:59:23.190
I would very much control everything

00:59:23.190 --> 00:59:26.600
in order to try to feel okay about it.

00:59:26.600 --> 00:59:29.040
And then at the end, if it went well,

00:59:29.040 --> 00:59:33.563
I would always, I realized that
I would thank my therapist.

00:59:35.430 --> 00:59:37.490
I don't know if this seems weird.

00:59:37.490 --> 00:59:41.140
Because I wanted to acknowledge to him

00:59:41.140 --> 00:59:45.580
kind of in like a code that
like, yes, you helped me.

00:59:45.580 --> 00:59:47.870
I got what I needed out of this.

00:59:47.870 --> 00:59:49.803
It's like kinda giving him a review,

00:59:50.640 --> 00:59:53.563
and demonstrating that I've made progress.

00:59:54.590 --> 00:59:55.520
And I did.

00:59:55.520 --> 00:59:57.210
So all of these things,

00:59:57.210 --> 00:59:58.500
I'm starting to realize,

00:59:58.500 --> 00:59:59.830
as we're having the conversation

00:59:59.830 --> 01:00:02.730
about how freaking difficult it is for me,

01:00:02.730 --> 01:00:06.350
and how much I put on myself
and bring into therapy

01:00:06.350 --> 01:00:08.383
just because it's therapy,

01:00:10.300 --> 01:00:12.300
or what I think it should be in my mind.

01:00:13.400 --> 01:00:16.363
And then I realized that like, I haven't,

