01:12:37.220 --> 01:12:40.280
So it's not like just
identifying the issue solves it

01:12:40.280 --> 01:12:42.743
because I still have all the tendencies,

01:12:43.880 --> 01:12:47.740
but it didn't take, it took two
sessions to get to the root,

01:12:47.740 --> 01:12:49.490
not two years to get to the root.

01:12:49.490 --> 01:12:51.843
Yeah, and like, I feel like we're like,

01:12:52.700 --> 01:12:56.970
we're certainly digging
into in the right place,

01:12:56.970 --> 01:13:01.060
but, and there's reasons why
maybe it's taking this long

01:13:01.060 --> 01:13:02.440
to get back to it.

01:13:02.440 --> 01:13:07.440
But I feel like that I can't
continue the route analogy

01:13:09.730 --> 01:13:12.030
in talking about the
point I'm trying to make.

01:13:13.502 --> 01:13:14.810
What is the point I'm trying to make?

01:13:14.810 --> 01:13:15.643
Well, okay.

01:13:17.430 --> 01:13:20.960
I'll talk a little bit
about, because I recent-

01:13:20.960 --> 01:13:22.130
Oh, I know what I was trying to say.

01:13:22.130 --> 01:13:23.770
We're looking in the right place,

01:13:23.770 --> 01:13:26.830
like saying, this is
the root of the issue.

01:13:26.830 --> 01:13:29.870
And I'm envious of the way that like,

01:13:29.870 --> 01:13:33.340
now you kinda know, well, I'm envious.

01:13:33.340 --> 01:13:35.760
I feel like you've
described an assignment.

01:13:35.760 --> 01:13:37.910
Like you bring the therapy your life,

01:13:37.910 --> 01:13:39.930
and then you make sure
that you're processing it

01:13:39.930 --> 01:13:43.070
on a level that you haven't been able to

01:13:43.070 --> 01:13:46.610
until therapy unlocked that
for you, an emotional level.

01:13:46.610 --> 01:13:50.650
So the protocol seems clear to me.

01:13:50.650 --> 01:13:52.350
Again, it's a plan.

01:13:52.350 --> 01:13:53.880
I feel like,

01:13:53.880 --> 01:13:58.040
and so is my exercise to find someone new

01:13:58.040 --> 01:13:59.900
who can give me that breakthrough,

01:13:59.900 --> 01:14:04.000
and then filter every
experience that I have

01:14:04.000 --> 01:14:08.520
through the trauma that
I've endured as a child,

01:14:08.520 --> 01:14:11.650
that then is making me
wanting to control everything

01:14:11.650 --> 01:14:12.740
to soothe it,

01:14:12.740 --> 01:14:17.740
or is my exercise that and
also letting go of the control

01:14:20.180 --> 01:14:22.939
of needing a plan to get
there, and analyzing-

01:14:22.939 --> 01:14:26.461
That's what makes it
really complicated for you.

01:14:26.461 --> 01:14:30.330
And in a different way, again,

01:14:30.330 --> 01:14:34.000
because I like to present
myself as having it together,

01:14:34.000 --> 01:14:36.920
and I do it unconsciously.

01:14:36.920 --> 01:14:38.570
Again, I don't know.

01:14:38.570 --> 01:14:42.260
Now, I'm not gonna get
into what I've explored

01:14:42.260 --> 01:14:45.810
specifically about my childhood

01:14:45.810 --> 01:14:48.600
that kind of potentially
led me to a place that,

01:14:48.600 --> 01:14:51.290
this is the way that I cope with things.

01:14:51.290 --> 01:14:56.290
But because I present as
someone who's got it together,

01:14:56.510 --> 01:14:57.603
as a default,

01:14:59.300 --> 01:15:03.390
sometimes it takes 30
minutes of talking in therapy

01:15:03.390 --> 01:15:05.750
to begin to break through the shell

01:15:05.750 --> 01:15:08.020
'cause I'm doing it
without even knowing it.

01:15:08.020 --> 01:15:11.393
Just like, I think things
are going pretty good,

01:15:12.670 --> 01:15:13.870
but they're not.

01:15:13.870 --> 01:15:16.020
Like and they may be externally,

01:15:16.020 --> 01:15:17.160
but like in here,

01:15:17.160 --> 01:15:19.399
no, there's something
that you're not really.

01:15:19.399 --> 01:15:20.890
I gotta bring that out.

01:15:20.890 --> 01:15:23.830
So yeah, there is this, I'm constantly.

01:15:23.830 --> 01:15:26.550
What I'm saying is that my
issue does directly impact

01:15:26.550 --> 01:15:28.310
the way that I do therapy.

01:15:28.310 --> 01:15:29.300
So I'm not saying that like,

01:15:29.300 --> 01:15:33.400
every time I start a therapy
session, I'm like, here we go.

01:15:33.400 --> 01:15:35.010
I know what the work is. Let's do it.

01:15:35.010 --> 01:15:37.730
No, the work is directly related

01:15:37.730 --> 01:15:41.510
into the way that I approach
therapy in a very similar way,

01:15:41.510 --> 01:15:43.460
just a different issue.

01:15:43.460 --> 01:15:47.120
But I feel like my therapist is so attuned

01:15:47.120 --> 01:15:50.590
to that being the problem
that he will just be like,

01:15:50.590 --> 01:15:52.650
okay, let's stop a second.

01:15:52.650 --> 01:15:54.900
Now, do you see what you did there?

01:15:54.900 --> 01:15:57.300
You've been talking about
this for this much time,

01:15:57.300 --> 01:15:59.700
and you see how quickly
you moved to this thing.

01:16:00.700 --> 01:16:02.840
But what about this thing?

01:16:02.840 --> 01:16:05.900
And so, because he's like,
I'm dealing with a guy,

01:16:05.900 --> 01:16:08.360
if it was like he was a
coach, and it was just like,

01:16:08.360 --> 01:16:11.010
the thing this guy can't do
is he can't go to his left.

01:16:11.010 --> 01:16:11.930
Right.

01:16:11.930 --> 01:16:12.763
Then he would be-

01:16:12.763 --> 01:16:13.830
See, there's a level of authority

01:16:13.830 --> 01:16:17.150
that I'm envious of that too,

01:16:17.150 --> 01:16:17.990
because I feel like-

01:16:17.990 --> 01:16:19.290
You don't feel like he's assertive enough.

01:16:19.290 --> 01:16:20.123
Yeah.

01:16:20.123 --> 01:16:21.640
Like he's like stop.

01:16:21.640 --> 01:16:24.360
When you said that, could that mean this?

01:16:24.360 --> 01:16:27.020
It's like, I just don't.

01:16:27.020 --> 01:16:28.470
I feel like it's all up to me.

01:16:28.470 --> 01:16:29.520
It's like, he's gonna answer.

01:16:29.520 --> 01:16:30.670
He's gonna ask a question,

01:16:30.670 --> 01:16:33.170
and he's gonna, what else am I gonna say?

01:16:33.170 --> 01:16:34.470
Now what am I gonna say to the wall?

01:16:34.470 --> 01:16:36.900
The fact that you've now
referred to him as a wall

01:16:36.900 --> 01:16:39.370
multiple times, maybe.

01:16:39.370 --> 01:16:41.660
Listen, I don't want, again,

01:16:41.660 --> 01:16:43.350
everybody's experience is different,

01:16:43.350 --> 01:16:46.100
but because there's a
question in your mind-

01:16:46.100 --> 01:16:47.440
But I think it's a style thing.

01:16:47.440 --> 01:16:50.600
I don't think it's a criticism
of him as much as like,

01:16:50.600 --> 01:16:52.410
am I discovering what I need?

01:16:52.410 --> 01:16:54.690
And 'cause it's just a relationship.

01:16:54.690 --> 01:16:56.730
So it's the style, right?

01:16:56.730 --> 01:16:58.960
The therapist is a person.

01:16:58.960 --> 01:17:01.090
So I just, I'm really feeling like

01:17:01.090 --> 01:17:02.770
I'm wanna try somebody else

01:17:02.770 --> 01:17:06.890
just because it's just a fit.

01:17:06.890 --> 01:17:08.260
It's a style. It's a-

01:17:08.260 --> 01:17:09.605
You wanna go to my therapist too?

01:17:10.870 --> 01:17:12.453
No, that would be unethical.

01:17:13.451 --> 01:17:15.690
At some point, the circle
has to stop expanding.

01:17:15.690 --> 01:17:17.513
I mean, could it be a handoff?

01:17:18.540 --> 01:17:19.373
Like-

01:17:19.373 --> 01:17:20.610
Maybe you take the weeks
that I'm not there.

01:17:20.610 --> 01:17:25.610
No, again, this is two very,
very non-professional guys

01:17:25.660 --> 01:17:26.560
talking about therapy,

01:17:26.560 --> 01:17:31.560
but I think, the only thing
that I want to affirm is that

01:17:31.680 --> 01:17:33.860
I don't think it's wrong to think,

01:17:33.860 --> 01:17:36.150
should I just, could I
try something else out?

01:17:36.150 --> 01:17:37.200
I know it's not wrong,

01:17:37.200 --> 01:17:39.080
and I know that they do it all the time.

01:17:39.080 --> 01:17:39.913
Yeah.

01:17:39.913 --> 01:17:43.060
And, but it's like, I get hung up on it.

01:17:43.060 --> 01:17:46.180
And I'm definitely thinking
that I'm not gonna tell him.

01:17:46.180 --> 01:17:49.510
I'm just gonna get a second
therapist at the same time.

01:17:49.510 --> 01:17:50.343
Wow.

01:17:51.334 --> 01:17:53.690
I don't know how I feel about that.

01:17:53.690 --> 01:17:54.690
I should be able to tell him.

01:17:54.690 --> 01:17:56.150
He's my therapist.

01:17:56.150 --> 01:17:56.983
Yeah, I think-

01:17:56.983 --> 01:17:58.140
But it's like, hey, I'm
breaking up with you

01:17:58.140 --> 01:17:59.720
for a little time until I change my mind

01:17:59.720 --> 01:18:01.360
and realized that the
grass wasn't greener,

01:18:01.360 --> 01:18:02.500
and then I'll come back to you.

01:18:02.500 --> 01:18:03.600
He knows all of that.

01:18:03.600 --> 01:18:05.420
It happens all the time, right?

01:18:05.420 --> 01:18:07.390
I can just be like, 'cause-

01:18:07.390 --> 01:18:08.890
I think you have to
talk to him about this.

01:18:08.890 --> 01:18:10.343
The temptation is,

01:18:12.660 --> 01:18:14.240
I'll just go to every other week,

01:18:14.240 --> 01:18:16.193
and then I can hedge with somebody else.

01:18:16.193 --> 01:18:17.790
I think the whole point is, is that-

01:18:17.790 --> 01:18:19.210
I gotta build the other person back up

01:18:19.210 --> 01:18:20.043
while I taper the other guy off.

01:18:20.043 --> 01:18:21.540
Well, I feel like-

01:18:21.540 --> 01:18:22.620
I don't wanna be left in a lurch.

01:18:22.620 --> 01:18:25.400
This is the thing I was kinda
getting at earlier is that

01:18:25.400 --> 01:18:28.640
I feel like there's the real narrative

01:18:28.640 --> 01:18:30.813
that's happening in here,

01:18:30.813 --> 01:18:32.600
and then there's the curated narrative

01:18:32.600 --> 01:18:33.980
that you bring to your therapist.

01:18:33.980 --> 01:18:36.640
And I think that, and I do the same thing.

01:18:36.640 --> 01:18:37.473
But the-

01:18:37.473 --> 01:18:39.190
I didn't know you were saying that.

01:18:39.190 --> 01:18:40.670
But what I'm, 'cause no,

01:18:40.670 --> 01:18:43.110
'cause I'm performative
in a different way, right?

01:18:43.110 --> 01:18:45.180
I'm definitely performative. Okay.

01:18:45.180 --> 01:18:46.013
But you're-

01:18:46.013 --> 01:18:48.590
My performative is that
I'm getting, I'm being-

01:18:48.590 --> 01:18:50.310
You're trying to control it,
and get the most out of it,

01:18:50.310 --> 01:18:51.710
and make it the best possible session.

01:18:51.710 --> 01:18:53.700
I'm just trying to appear
like I got it together.

01:18:53.700 --> 01:18:55.760
Yeah.
Even if I don't.

01:18:55.760 --> 01:18:57.620
But they're both performative
in a certain way.

01:18:57.620 --> 01:19:00.200
So the thing that he breaks me out of is

01:19:00.200 --> 01:19:02.490
the curated narrative.

01:19:02.490 --> 01:19:06.770
And either you gotta break yourself

01:19:06.770 --> 01:19:08.310
out of the curated narrative or-

01:19:08.310 --> 01:19:09.960
Or if I don't have a curated narrative,

01:19:09.960 --> 01:19:12.703
whatever it is, I just
want to have more of a,

01:19:13.770 --> 01:19:18.540
I think his style is more
like raise a question,

01:19:18.540 --> 01:19:20.070
and then it's self-discovery,

01:19:20.070 --> 01:19:23.150
but it's not a conversation.

01:19:23.150 --> 01:19:25.700
We're not batting ideas back and forth.

01:19:25.700 --> 01:19:26.533
Well, and you know-

01:19:26.533 --> 01:19:28.550
And I kinda want a little more of that.

01:19:28.550 --> 01:19:31.320
This is a way that we tell
people who work for us

01:19:31.320 --> 01:19:32.760
and work with us this all the time.

01:19:32.760 --> 01:19:35.340
Because if there's anything that we are,

01:19:35.340 --> 01:19:38.430
we have very much in common in
the way we work with people,

01:19:38.430 --> 01:19:42.340
is we invite collaboration
really intensely.

01:19:42.340 --> 01:19:44.810
And we tell people, when we
get ready to work on a project,

01:19:44.810 --> 01:19:47.880
we say, listen, you can't offend us,

01:19:47.880 --> 01:19:50.450
and we don't hold any ideas sacred.

01:19:50.450 --> 01:19:53.590
If you don't like something,
you should tell us

01:19:53.590 --> 01:19:54.840
because that's how we work.

01:19:54.840 --> 01:19:56.670
And listen, if you step back,

01:19:56.670 --> 01:19:58.700
and you make it a light touch thing,

01:19:58.700 --> 01:20:00.630
and you don't ever voice your opinion,

01:20:00.630 --> 01:20:01.850
we will bowl over you

01:20:01.850 --> 01:20:05.060
because we have so much
intense creative energy.

01:20:05.060 --> 01:20:06.630
Now, I actually think

01:20:06.630 --> 01:20:09.110
we kind of function
relationships in this way.

01:20:09.110 --> 01:20:10.800
And so I think what
you're asking, it's like-

01:20:10.800 --> 01:20:12.310
When I have something to bring to therapy-

01:20:12.310 --> 01:20:13.290
You want him to-

01:20:13.290 --> 01:20:15.510
I bowl over. I talk to the wall.

01:20:15.510 --> 01:20:16.960
Yeah, you want him to reach out

01:20:16.960 --> 01:20:18.670
and grab you by the collar.

01:20:18.670 --> 01:20:20.270
Yeah.
So to speak.

01:20:20.270 --> 01:20:22.190
And again, I feel like
my therapist does that

01:20:22.190 --> 01:20:25.940
in the most respectful,
friendly way possible,

01:20:25.940 --> 01:20:28.240
but he just kinda is ready to,

01:20:28.240 --> 01:20:32.770
when he smells bullshit,
he says, I smell bullshit.

01:20:32.770 --> 01:20:33.730
You know what I mean?

01:20:33.730 --> 01:20:35.490
I don't know what he need to smell.

01:20:35.490 --> 01:20:38.550
Maybe there's a different scent for me,

01:20:38.550 --> 01:20:40.070
a different stink than bullshit.

01:20:40.070 --> 01:20:43.090
But yeah, it's not, whatever that is,

01:20:43.090 --> 01:20:45.210
I would like to be-

01:20:45.210 --> 01:20:46.610
So I think you gotta talk to him,

01:20:46.610 --> 01:20:48.610
and I think you gotta say that I'm just,

01:20:49.954 --> 01:20:51.310
this is how I feel.

01:20:51.310 --> 01:20:55.923
And what I want to do is
maybe meet with someone else

01:20:55.923 --> 01:20:57.930
just 'cause I just don't have anything,

01:20:57.930 --> 01:21:01.707
I don't have any sort of
connection to compare this to,

01:21:01.707 --> 01:21:02.864
and see what he says.

01:21:02.864 --> 01:21:03.920
And then I'm starting over.

01:21:03.920 --> 01:21:05.950
It's like, that's why I haven't done it.

01:21:05.950 --> 01:21:09.313
And that's when things go better,

01:21:11.020 --> 01:21:13.010
and I have that, like I don't
have anything to talk about,

01:21:13.010 --> 01:21:15.093
I'm like, I'll just, I'll stretch it out.

01:21:15.950 --> 01:21:18.030
I'll just meet every other week.

01:21:18.030 --> 01:21:18.863
We'll try that out.

01:21:18.863 --> 01:21:20.000
Maybe I don't need therapy.

01:21:20.000 --> 01:21:21.840
I start to tell myself that stuff.

01:21:21.840 --> 01:21:22.673
Okay.

01:21:22.673 --> 01:21:23.879
And then I know what-

01:21:23.879 --> 01:21:25.610
This is what I wanted to talk about

01:21:25.610 --> 01:21:28.163
because I started thinking
the same thing, okay?

01:21:30.936 --> 01:21:32.020
And I actually asked Jesse

01:21:32.020 --> 01:21:34.150
when we were getting
ready for this episode.

01:21:34.150 --> 01:21:38.937
I was like, I feel like I
understand what my problem is,

01:21:38.937 --> 01:21:41.470
and I understand what I'm,

01:21:41.470 --> 01:21:43.600
kind of what I'm doing to work on it,

01:21:43.600 --> 01:21:46.230
but it's really difficult for me to talk,

01:21:46.230 --> 01:21:50.490
to articulate the progress
and what's actually changed.

01:21:50.490 --> 01:21:52.478
And she was like, well, do
you want me to tell you?

01:21:52.478 --> 01:21:53.347
Right?

01:21:53.347 --> 01:21:55.200
And so I actually wrote
down some of the stuff

01:21:55.200 --> 01:21:57.660
that she said, 'cause I
asked her to repeat it.

01:21:57.660 --> 01:21:58.493
Okay.

01:21:59.850 --> 01:22:01.830
First of all, she kinda laughed again.

01:22:01.830 --> 01:22:04.170
And it was like, what'd
you want me to tell you

01:22:04.170 --> 01:22:05.030
what I've seen?

01:22:05.030 --> 01:22:09.053
She's like, you're much more
in touch with your emotions,

01:22:10.060 --> 01:22:13.400
which has translated into
the way that we communicate,

01:22:13.400 --> 01:22:14.603
me and her communicate.

01:22:16.260 --> 01:22:17.200
Been married for 20 years.

01:22:17.200 --> 01:22:18.870
Spent many of those years,

01:22:18.870 --> 01:22:20.840
if we were arguing about something,

01:22:20.840 --> 01:22:24.200
my main goal was to get a
point across, to debate,

01:22:24.200 --> 01:22:27.540
to be like, this is why I think
that I'm right about this,

01:22:27.540 --> 01:22:30.410
and these are my supporting,
this is the supporting evidence

01:22:32.860 --> 01:22:35.560
versus worrying about connecting.

01:22:35.560 --> 01:22:36.520
Do you know what I'm saying?

01:22:36.520 --> 01:22:39.050
Or just saying, this is how I feel.

01:22:39.050 --> 01:22:43.160
Like I've literally changed
the way that we argue

01:22:43.160 --> 01:22:45.550
about things by saying,

01:22:45.550 --> 01:22:48.427
well, this is what I'm feeling right now.

01:22:48.427 --> 01:22:51.385
And it isn't, I feel that
you're a jerk about it.

01:22:51.385 --> 01:22:53.500
It's just like, no, this
is the emotional response

01:22:53.500 --> 01:22:54.590
that I'm having right now.

01:22:54.590 --> 01:22:56.430
And she's already there
because she's been doing,

01:22:56.430 --> 01:22:58.130
and that's completely changed the way,

01:22:58.130 --> 01:22:59.760
that's changed conflict resolution.

01:22:59.760 --> 01:23:02.220
I didn't even know that
until she pointed it out.

01:23:02.220 --> 01:23:05.515
She's like, you don't get mad as much.

01:23:05.515 --> 01:23:08.660
I mean, again, that's something
that I didn't really have.

01:23:08.660 --> 01:23:10.789
You really don't have a way

01:23:10.789 --> 01:23:12.693
to perceive yourself in that way.

01:23:14.090 --> 01:23:16.900
She said we are closer
than we've ever been.

01:23:16.900 --> 01:23:18.000
And I was like, well, you know what?

01:23:18.000 --> 01:23:21.120
I recently told somebody
that I thought that too.

01:23:21.120 --> 01:23:23.480
Oh, that has something to do with-

01:23:23.480 --> 01:23:24.550
Emotions.
Therapy.

01:23:24.550 --> 01:23:26.210
Yes, okay.

01:23:26.210 --> 01:23:28.990
And then she says, and
think about the way that we,

01:23:28.990 --> 01:23:31.610
the differences in the way
that we deal with our kids

01:23:31.610 --> 01:23:33.650
since before therapy.

01:23:33.650 --> 01:23:38.560
One of the things that our
therapist has really harped on

01:23:38.560 --> 01:23:41.030
is this maintaining the relationship

01:23:41.030 --> 01:23:43.260
with our kids as they age.

01:23:43.260 --> 01:23:46.270
And as they get older
and get more independent,

01:23:46.270 --> 01:23:49.090
Locke will be in his
senior year in high school,

01:23:49.090 --> 01:23:52.420
resisting the urge to
datafy every situation

01:23:52.420 --> 01:23:55.100
and to moralize, and to
correct, and to teach,

01:23:55.100 --> 01:23:57.190
and to use every conversation
as an opportunity

01:23:57.190 --> 01:23:59.530
to give my wise perspective,

01:23:59.530 --> 01:24:02.080
versus using every
conversation as an opportunity

01:24:02.080 --> 01:24:04.143
to connect emotionally.

01:24:05.000 --> 01:24:07.596
And again, that's just not something that,

01:24:07.596 --> 01:24:09.320
I love my kids.

01:24:09.320 --> 01:24:10.680
I love being with my kids.

01:24:10.680 --> 01:24:13.573
I love doing things and
having fun with my kids.

01:24:14.560 --> 01:24:17.340
But usually if they start
talking about something,

01:24:17.340 --> 01:24:21.720
my default is to be like
robot analyze situation,

01:24:21.720 --> 01:24:22.630
give solution.

01:24:22.630 --> 01:24:24.310
Like, it's just my personality.

01:24:24.310 --> 01:24:25.580
You bring me a problem,

01:24:25.580 --> 01:24:27.860
I'll come up with a solution real fast.

01:24:27.860 --> 01:24:30.020
Versus bring me a problem,

01:24:30.020 --> 01:24:31.810
and let me get into,

01:24:31.810 --> 01:24:33.160
let me get behind that problem with you.

01:24:33.160 --> 01:24:33.993
Let's not solve it.

01:24:33.993 --> 01:24:35.610
Let's just agree that it's a problem

01:24:35.610 --> 01:24:36.760
and connect emotionally.

01:24:36.760 --> 01:24:39.400
These are things that have happened,

01:24:39.400 --> 01:24:41.880
but I needed my wife to point them out.

01:24:41.880 --> 01:24:45.200
So I guess my question for you is,

01:24:45.200 --> 01:24:47.310
I think before you make
any of this decision,

01:24:47.310 --> 01:24:48.920
you gotta talk to Christy about it.

01:24:48.920 --> 01:24:50.510
Oh, sure. We're already talking about.

01:24:50.510 --> 01:24:53.850
Yeah, but like if she were to be like,

01:24:53.850 --> 01:24:58.850
oh no, like I've seen this
change in you in these ways,

01:25:00.680 --> 01:25:03.180
or be like, no, I think
you need a new therapist.

01:25:03.180 --> 01:25:05.640
Like, she's gonna know way better than me.

01:25:05.640 --> 01:25:07.240
We had a fight last night, so.

01:25:08.183 --> 01:25:09.820
But we're having a date tonight.

01:25:09.820 --> 01:25:10.653
Okay.

01:25:10.653 --> 01:25:12.667
Just gotta get the fight out of the way.

01:25:12.667 --> 01:25:16.150
But the fight was, the
conflict was resolved,

01:25:16.150 --> 01:25:18.910
but I did not, I kind of showed my ass.

01:25:18.910 --> 01:25:21.160
So I didn't really
demonstrate a lot of progress

01:25:21.160 --> 01:25:22.660
in any area.

01:25:22.660 --> 01:25:27.113
So I don't know if tonight
is a good night to ask.

01:25:28.200 --> 01:25:32.780
'Cause she might be like,
there's work to be done.

01:25:32.780 --> 01:25:36.170
I mean, that is the sense I get.

01:25:36.170 --> 01:25:39.370
It's like the, and in an
effort to wrap this up,

01:25:39.370 --> 01:25:41.760
I think just the benefit
of kinda looking back.

01:25:41.760 --> 01:25:44.960
The other thing was that
I looked back at my notes,

01:25:44.960 --> 01:25:47.070
and they ended a year ago.

01:25:47.070 --> 01:25:48.693
I stopped jotting down.

01:25:48.693 --> 01:25:51.040
I think I wanna start that back up

01:25:51.040 --> 01:25:54.410
because I'm sure Christy
can give me perspective

01:25:54.410 --> 01:25:58.800
on all of this, and I'll
put that question to her

01:25:58.800 --> 01:26:03.800
in terms of like how I've
changed or grown, if at all.

01:26:04.210 --> 01:26:06.603
I know, I believe I have.

01:26:08.020 --> 01:26:10.070
I've definitely survived a
lot of things I couldn't,

01:26:10.070 --> 01:26:11.180
and that is saying a lot,

01:26:11.180 --> 01:26:12.761
and it's not something to diminish.

01:26:12.761 --> 01:26:16.740
You've had acute situations

01:26:16.740 --> 01:26:18.950
that took a lot of energy and time.

01:26:18.950 --> 01:26:23.230
But I do think I wanna
keep writing things down,

01:26:23.230 --> 01:26:24.880
and I am gonna seriously
consider getting a new therapist.

01:26:24.880 --> 01:26:26.960
And I think that came from within me.

01:26:26.960 --> 01:26:29.183
That's not something that you
were, that wasn't your idea.

01:26:29.183 --> 01:26:31.433
Does your therapist
listen to this podcast?

01:26:35.520 --> 01:26:36.353
Why would he?

01:26:36.353 --> 01:26:37.186
I don't think my therapist,

01:26:37.186 --> 01:26:39.350
my therapist doesn't listen to it

01:26:39.350 --> 01:26:42.200
because every time he refers
to it, he calls it earbuds.

01:26:43.075 --> 01:26:44.020
So.

01:26:44.020 --> 01:26:44.853
Yeah, yeah.

01:26:44.853 --> 01:26:46.225
Just keep it that way.

01:26:46.225 --> 01:26:48.489
I don't think he would.

01:26:48.489 --> 01:26:49.380
And I do not correct him

01:26:49.380 --> 01:26:52.130
because it's my way to know
if he's actually listening.

01:26:55.040 --> 01:26:58.333
Boy, this is, therapy's tough.

01:26:59.360 --> 01:27:00.193
What have we learned?

01:27:00.193 --> 01:27:02.080
Is this a ringing endorsement.

01:27:02.080 --> 01:27:06.860
No, to me, and it's funny 'cause we,

01:27:06.860 --> 01:27:09.230
I've had two conversations
in the past month

01:27:09.230 --> 01:27:11.360
with two close friends,

01:27:11.360 --> 01:27:13.400
one who just started therapy

01:27:13.400 --> 01:27:16.150
and feels the need to change therapist,

01:27:16.150 --> 01:27:17.660
and one who just started therapist

01:27:17.660 --> 01:27:20.580
and wanted to check in with
me after a few sessions

01:27:20.580 --> 01:27:21.950
and just ask the question.

01:27:21.950 --> 01:27:25.750
Like, it's hard to evaluate.

01:27:25.750 --> 01:27:29.400
And he was thinking about it
from a financial perspective,

01:27:29.400 --> 01:27:30.620
which is a note we wanna make.

01:27:30.620 --> 01:27:35.370
I mean, being able to pay
for your own mental health

01:27:36.260 --> 01:27:37.843
is a privilege that we have.

01:27:37.843 --> 01:27:40.700
It's the luxury, sadly.

01:27:40.700 --> 01:27:42.430
Sadly, that's the case.

01:27:42.430 --> 01:27:44.510
But we were talking to
edits before the show

01:27:44.510 --> 01:27:45.370
with Kiko and Jenna,

01:27:45.370 --> 01:27:48.350
and they were both kind of
reminding us about like,

01:27:48.350 --> 01:27:53.350
there are mental health
resources and groups,

01:27:53.960 --> 01:27:56.380
and there are actually,
there's free resources

01:27:56.380 --> 01:27:59.567
depending on your background
and things that you deal with.

01:27:59.567 --> 01:28:01.080
And the community you live in.

01:28:01.080 --> 01:28:01.913
Yeah.
How big it it.

01:28:01.913 --> 01:28:04.593
There are ways to get
at some of the stuff.

01:28:05.450 --> 01:28:08.120
There's affinity groups like LGBTQ.

01:28:08.120 --> 01:28:09.340
I mean, if that's.

01:28:09.340 --> 01:28:14.220
Or there's support groups.

01:28:14.220 --> 01:28:16.480
Yeah, and sometimes that can
happen in a group setting

01:28:16.480 --> 01:28:18.890
and doesn't necessarily
have to be one-on-one.

01:28:18.890 --> 01:28:23.670
But if you can afford it,
try to make it happen.

01:28:23.670 --> 01:28:25.180
But he was asking that
question 'cause he was like,

01:28:25.180 --> 01:28:27.600
I'm spending a fair
amount of money on this,

01:28:27.600 --> 01:28:28.433
and just like what?

01:28:28.433 --> 01:28:31.420
And I find myself, like he was,

01:28:31.420 --> 01:28:33.090
he's a lot, he's not like you,

01:28:33.090 --> 01:28:34.230
but it was funny 'cause he did something

01:28:34.230 --> 01:28:35.063
that you would have done,

01:28:35.063 --> 01:28:38.630
which is he printed out
like a summary of himself

01:28:38.630 --> 01:28:40.070
with background information,

01:28:40.070 --> 01:28:41.740
like a resume that you give to a therapist

01:28:41.740 --> 01:28:44.110
and send it to the therapist
before the appointment.

01:28:44.110 --> 01:28:44.943
Yeah.

01:28:44.943 --> 01:28:45.776
To kind of get it out of the way

01:28:45.776 --> 01:28:47.280
so they didn't have to spend
a bunch of time talking.

01:28:47.280 --> 01:28:48.113
I love that idea.

01:28:48.113 --> 01:28:49.620
And he was like, the therapist was like,

01:28:49.620 --> 01:28:51.650
no one has ever done this.

01:28:51.650 --> 01:28:53.750
I don't know how to feel
about it, but thank you.

01:28:53.750 --> 01:28:54.980
Really?

01:28:54.980 --> 01:28:56.580
So, but I told him the same thing.

01:28:56.580 --> 01:28:59.083
I was like, listen, man,
like I had to ask Jesse.

01:29:01.048 --> 01:29:03.440
'Cause I can't immediately articulate.

01:29:03.440 --> 01:29:06.160
You forget what's actually happening.

01:29:06.160 --> 01:29:08.040
Therapy rolls around and I'm just like,

01:29:08.040 --> 01:29:09.800
ah, man, I just wanna watch TV.

01:29:09.800 --> 01:29:13.600
I don't find myself longing for
my next therapy appointment.

01:29:13.600 --> 01:29:16.820
If I have to miss it, and I
end up going a full month,

01:29:16.820 --> 01:29:20.200
I don't feel the need-

01:29:20.200 --> 01:29:21.520
You get secretly happy.

01:29:21.520 --> 01:29:22.860
No, I don't get secretly happy,

01:29:22.860 --> 01:29:24.530
but I don't have this felt need of like

01:29:24.530 --> 01:29:26.730
all this stuff building
up that's about to explode

01:29:26.730 --> 01:29:29.250
the moment that the Zoom call starts.

01:29:29.250 --> 01:29:32.220
But then what I find is that
when the Zoom call does start,

01:29:32.220 --> 01:29:33.760
and I just start letting it flow-

01:29:33.760 --> 01:29:35.440
It's there. That's your MO.

01:29:35.440 --> 01:29:36.610
It's there. Right.

01:29:36.610 --> 01:29:37.443
Yeah.
So-

01:29:38.400 --> 01:29:39.540
So you're recommending therapy.

01:29:39.540 --> 01:29:41.280
I'm recommending therapy,

01:29:41.280 --> 01:29:44.090
but it's not, yeah, it's not
gonna be like some easy thing.

01:29:44.090 --> 01:29:47.230
My actual recommendation,
to wrap things up,

01:29:47.230 --> 01:29:50.377
is a book that I've been reading called

01:29:50.377 --> 01:29:53.190
"Why We Sleep" by, I think
his name is Michael Walker.

01:29:53.190 --> 01:29:55.400
Let me just make sure that's his name.

01:29:55.400 --> 01:29:56.533
Matthew Walker.

01:29:58.610 --> 01:30:01.530
This was like a New York Times
bestseller a few years ago.

01:30:01.530 --> 01:30:05.990
And it's basically just all about sleep

01:30:05.990 --> 01:30:08.190
and the importance of sleep.

01:30:08.190 --> 01:30:10.690
Now I will say that, as I've
dug into it a little bit,

01:30:10.690 --> 01:30:11.940
that there are some people,

01:30:11.940 --> 01:30:12.773
some researchers out there who question

01:30:12.773 --> 01:30:15.130
some of the conclusions he comes to.

01:30:15.130 --> 01:30:16.629
Okay.

01:30:16.629 --> 01:30:20.490
But so much of the book
is him just saying,

01:30:20.490 --> 01:30:23.680
this was a study, a peer
reviewed study that was done

01:30:23.680 --> 01:30:25.760
with these people, and this is what it.

01:30:25.760 --> 01:30:27.670
He explores all these
different aspects of sleep,

01:30:27.670 --> 01:30:31.330
and you will learn a lot
of really good information.

01:30:31.330 --> 01:30:35.800
But I've just been absolutely blown away

01:30:35.800 --> 01:30:40.610
at how, just reminded at how important

01:30:40.610 --> 01:30:41.840
getting a good night's sleep is.

01:30:41.840 --> 01:30:43.010
Like, if you don't wanna read the book,

01:30:43.010 --> 01:30:44.860
then just get a good night's sleep.

01:30:44.860 --> 01:30:47.313
Get seven to nine hours of
sleep every single night.

01:30:47.313 --> 01:30:49.210
That would be my recommendation.

01:30:49.210 --> 01:30:51.070
I like that.

01:30:51.070 --> 01:30:53.720
But if you're interested in
that kind of information-

01:30:53.720 --> 01:30:55.680
If I can sleep with my therapist,

01:30:55.680 --> 01:30:56.830
maybe all my problems will be-

01:30:56.830 --> 01:30:57.663
Right.

01:30:57.663 --> 01:30:58.496
And I don't mean like that.

01:30:58.496 --> 01:30:59.500
Yep, oh, I understand.

01:30:59.500 --> 01:31:01.270
But it's just, it's a good book in general

01:31:01.270 --> 01:31:04.750
